/* EU learn notes — a short educational paragraph per EU question,
   shown after answering so each item teaches, not just tests.
   Generated per topic; keys must match ids in data/eu.js. */
"use strict";

const EU_LEARN = {
  "eu001": "The right of initiative is anchored in Article 17 TEU and is often called the Commission's most powerful tool, since nothing enters the ordinary legislative procedure without a Commission text. Under Article 225 TFEU Parliament, and under Article 241 TFEU the Council, can only request a proposal, and a European Citizens' Initiative with one million signatures can likewise merely invite one.",
  "eu002": "Treaty rules cap the Parliament at 750 members plus the President and use degressive proportionality: bigger states get more seats but fewer per capita, with Germany at the 96-seat maximum and Malta, Luxembourg and Cyprus at the 6-seat minimum. MEPs have been directly elected every five years since the first election in 1979.",
  "eu003": "The single-seat question is a classic exam trap: Protocol 6 to the treaties fixes Strasbourg as the seat, and the Court of Justice confirmed in 2012 that all twelve monthly plenaries must be held there. Strasbourg was chosen as a symbol of Franco-German reconciliation, and moving the seat would require unanimous treaty-level agreement, which France blocks.",
  "eu004": "A third body often confused with both is the Council of Europe, a separate 46-member international organisation in Strasbourg that runs the European Court of Human Rights. The European Council became a formal EU institution only with the Lisbon Treaty in 2009, though leaders' summits had existed since 1974; it meets at least four times a year in Brussels.",
  "eu005": "The double majority applies since 1 November 2014, replacing the old system of weighted votes agreed at Nice. Remember the concrete numbers for 27 members: 15 states and 65 percent of population to pass, and a blocking minority must include at least four states so that three big countries alone cannot block. Around 80 percent of EU legislation is adopted by QMV.",
  "eu006": "Von der Leyen, a former German defence minister, became the first woman to head the Commission in 2019, when she was confirmed by only nine votes; in July 2024 she won 401 votes. Her first term was defined by the European Green Deal, NextGenerationEU and the pandemic response; the second emphasises competitiveness, defence and the Draghi report agenda.",
  "eu007": "Costa is the first European Council President from southern Europe and the first former prime minister of Portugal in the role; his predecessors were Herman Van Rompuy, Donald Tusk and Charles Michel. The post was created by the Lisbon Treaty in 2009, and the maximum time in office is five years (two terms of two and a half years).",
  "eu008": "The High Representative wears three hats worth memorising: chair of the Foreign Affairs Council, Vice-President of the Commission (hence HR/VP), and head of the European External Action Service created in 2010. The holder is appointed by the European Council with the agreement of the Commission President; predecessors were Solana, Ashton, Mogherini and Borrell.",
  "eu009": "The Nice and Lisbon treaties both foresaw fewer Commissioners than member states, and Lisbon set two-thirds of the number of states from 2014. The reduction was abandoned as part of the guarantees given to Ireland after its 2008 referendum rejected Lisbon; a 2013 European Council decision formally kept one national per state, and every enlargement adds a seat to the College.",
  "eu010": "The presidency order is fixed years ahead by a Council decision, and states work in 'trios' sharing an 18-month programme; Poland, Denmark and Cyprus form one trio. The presidency chairs most Council configurations and working parties, sets agendas and brokers compromises, but has no formal extra vote. Ireland last held the presidency in 2013.",
  "eu011": "This arrangement dates from the Lisbon Treaty, which split foreign policy out of the old General Affairs and External Relations Council. Also outside the rotation: the Eurogroup has its own elected president, and the European Council is chaired by its permanent President. Working parties preparing the Foreign Affairs Council are largely chaired by EEAS officials rather than the rotating presidency.",
  "eu012": "The CJEU comprises two courts since the Civil Service Tribunal was dissolved in 2016: the Court of Justice and the General Court, the latter enlarged to two judges per member state. Key procedures to know are preliminary rulings under Article 267 TFEU, infringement actions against member states, and annulment actions against EU acts. Do not confuse it with the ICJ in The Hague.",
  "eu013": "Created by the 1975 Budgetary Treaty, operational from October 1977 and made a full institution by the Maastricht Treaty, the ECA publishes an annual report and a 'statement of assurance' (DAS) on the reliability of EU accounts, which feeds into Parliament's discharge procedure for the budget. When auditors find suspected fraud they refer cases to OLAF or the EPPO, since the Court itself cannot prosecute.",
  "eu014": "The ECB was established in 1998 and is the centre of the Eurosystem (ECB plus euro-area national central banks); the ESCB also includes non-euro central banks. Since 2014 it also supervises the largest euro-area banks through the Single Supervisory Mechanism. Its independence is treaty-protected: it may neither seek nor take instructions from governments or EU institutions, and it cannot directly finance governments.",
  "eu015": "The procedure is in Article 17(7) TEU: the European Council decides by qualified majority, 'taking into account' the EP elections, and Parliament elects the candidate by a majority of its component members (currently 361 of 720). If the candidate fails, the European Council must propose a new name within one month. Individual Commissioners then face confirmation hearings before parliamentary committees.",
  "eu016": "The name comes from the French 'Comité des représentants permanents', established in 1958. COREPER II, the ambassadors themselves, handles political, financial and foreign affairs dossiers; COREPER I, the deputies, covers technical areas like environment and transport. Items agreed at this level pass through the Council as 'A points' without discussion, which is how most legislation is actually settled.",
  "eu017": "The office was created by the Maastricht Treaty and is based in Strasbourg. Maladministration covers unfairness, discrimination, abuse of power, failure to reply and refusal of information; the CJEU acting judicially is excluded. The Ombudsman can act on complaints from any citizen or resident or open own-initiative inquiries, and issues recommendations that are not legally binding but usually followed.",
  "eu018": "Both committees date from different eras: the EESC was created by the 1957 Treaty of Rome, while the CoR was added by Maastricht and first met in 1994. Each has 329 members appointed by the Council for five-year terms. The CoR can bring actions before the Court of Justice to defend its prerogatives and the subsidiarity principle, a power the EESC lacks.",
  "eu019": "A useful table of term lengths to memorise: Commission and Parliament five years; European Council President two and a half years renewable once; Eurogroup president two and a half years, re-electable without limit; CJEU judges and Advocates General six years renewable; Court of Auditors members six years renewable; ECB Executive Board eight years non-renewable; the ECB President's term is also eight years, non-renewable.",
  "eu020": "The censure motion, in Article 234 TFEU, has existed since the 1950s but has never passed; the closest case was 1999, when the Santer Commission resigned en masse before the vote to pre-empt likely defeat over fraud allegations. Censure can only topple the entire College, not a single Commissioner, though a President can request an individual's resignation.",
  "eu021": "The declaration was drafted largely by Jean Monnet and read out by French Foreign Minister Robert Schuman at the Quai d'Orsay in Paris. Its core logic was to make war between France and Germany 'not merely unthinkable, but materially impossible' by placing war-making industries under a common High Authority. Memorise the sequence: Schuman Declaration 1950, Treaty of Paris 1951, Treaties of Rome 1957.",
  "eu022": "Two treaties were actually signed in Rome on the same day, creating the EEC and Euratom, so exam questions may say 'Treaties of Rome' in the plural. The EEC treaty established a common market, a customs union and common policies such as agriculture and transport. Do not confuse it with the Treaty of Paris, which created only the coal and steel community.",
  "eu023": "A handy mnemonic is 'Benelux plus the big three': Belgium, the Netherlands and Luxembourg had already formed a customs union in 1944-48, and they joined France, West Germany and Italy. Note that the UK was invited to the negotiations but declined, preferring Commonwealth trade and later founding EFTA. Spain, under Franco's dictatorship, was politically ineligible until the 1980s.",
  "eu024": "Signed on 18 April 1951 and in force from 1952, the Treaty of Paris was unique in being concluded for a fixed 50-year term, unlike the Rome treaties, which are of unlimited duration. Its High Authority, first led by Jean Monnet, was the supranational forerunner of today's Commission. When it expired in 2002, coal and steel simply fell under the general EC treaty rules.",
  "eu025": "This enlargement took effect on 1 January 1973, raising membership from six to nine. It became possible only after Charles de Gaulle, who had vetoed British entry twice, resigned in 1969; his successor Georges Pompidou lifted the objection at the 1969 Hague summit. Remember the pattern of Norwegian referendums: voters rejected accession in both 1972 and 1994.",
  "eu026": "The SEA was the first major revision of the Rome treaties, in force from 1 July 1987, and followed the Commission's 1985 White Paper listing some 300 measures needed to remove barriers. It also gave the European Council treaty recognition, formalised European Political Cooperation on foreign policy, and introduced the cooperation procedure giving Parliament a stronger legislative voice.",
  "eu027": "Maastricht entered into force on 1 November 1993, delayed by a Danish 'no' in June 1992 that was reversed in 1993 after the Edinburgh opt-outs. Its three pillars were the Communities, the Common Foreign and Security Policy, and Justice and Home Affairs; Lisbon later abolished this structure. It also renamed the EEC simply the 'European Community' and created the Committee of the Regions.",
  "eu028": "Amsterdam, in force from 1 May 1999, also communitarised parts of justice and home affairs (visas, asylum, immigration), created the High Representative for the CFSP, and inserted a suspension mechanism for serious breaches of EU values, the ancestor of today's Article 7. Ireland's opt-out flowed from its Common Travel Area with the UK, which it wished to preserve.",
  "eu029": "Nice entered into force on 1 February 2003 after Ireland's second referendum. Its formula of re-weighted Council votes and capped Parliament seats was widely criticised as byzantine, which is why the Laeken Declaration launched a deeper reform debate almost immediately. Nice also allowed the Court of Justice to sit in a Grand Chamber and expanded enhanced cooperation rules.",
  "eu030": "Lisbon was signed on 13 December 2007 and entered into force on 1 December 2009, after Ireland's second referendum. It abolished the pillar structure, merged the Union and Community into a single legal entity, and doubled the High Representative as a Commission Vice-President heading the new European External Action Service. Herman Van Rompuy became the first permanent European Council President.",
  "eu031": "The 'big bang' of 1 May 2004 was the largest single enlargement in EU history, adding around 75 million people. Eight of the ten were former communist states, plus the two Mediterranean islands Malta and Cyprus. Cyprus joined as a divided island: the acquis is suspended in the Turkish-occupied north after Greek Cypriots rejected the UN's Annan reunification plan weeks before accession.",
  "eu032": "Croatia's accession followed the longest negotiations of any member at that point, slowed by cooperation issues with the ICTY war-crimes tribunal and a border dispute with Slovenia. Note the dual milestone of 1 January 2023, when Croatia simultaneously adopted the euro as its 20th member and joined Schengen, a combination no other country had achieved on the same day.",
  "eu033": "Before Lisbon there was no treaty exit clause at all, a point examiners like to test. The UK triggered Article 50 on 29 March 2017, and the two-year deadline was extended three times before departure. Remember that Article 7 TEU concerns suspension of rights for value breaches, and Article 258 TFEU is the infringement procedure, common distractors.",
  "eu034": "The TEU contains the Union's values, institutional basics and CFSP rules in just 55 articles, while the TFEU spells out competences, policies and procedures in 358 articles. Both have identical legal rank, as does the Charter of Fundamental Rights since Lisbon. Euratom survives as a legally separate Community with its own treaty, a nuance worth remembering.",
  "eu035": "The Constitutional Treaty was signed in Rome in October 2004 and had already been ratified by nine of the then twenty-five member states, including Spain by referendum, before the French and Dutch votes of May and June 2005 killed it; eighteen ratified in all before the process was abandoned. Lisbon deliberately dropped its state-like symbols, such as references to the flag, anthem and the title 'Minister for Foreign Affairs', to ease ratification.",
  "eu036": "A former French finance minister, Delors served an exceptional three terms and gave his name to the 1989 Delors Report, whose three-stage plan for monetary union was written into Maastricht. His famous 1988 prediction that most economic legislation would soon originate in Brussels galvanised both integrationists and Margaret Thatcher's opposing Bruges speech. He died in December 2023.",
  "eu037": "The June 1979 elections chose 410 MEPs, and Simone Veil, a French Holocaust survivor, was elected the assembly's first directly elected President, a favourite exam fact. Direct elections had been foreseen in the Treaty of Rome but were only enabled by a 1976 Council act. Turnout has been a recurring concern, falling steadily until it rebounded in 2019.",
  "eu038": "Denmark's opt-out was secured in the December 1992 Edinburgh Agreement after Danish voters had rejected Maastricht; a second referendum in May 1993 then passed. Danish voters explicitly refused the euro again in a 2000 referendum, yet the krone remains pegged to the euro through ERM II. Sweden, by contrast, simply avoids meeting the convergence criteria deliberately.",
  "eu039": "Georgia applied at the same time but initially received only a 'European perspective', being granted candidate status in December 2023, when the European Council also decided to open talks with Ukraine and Moldova. Bosnia and Herzegovina became a candidate in December 2022. Candidate status is decided by the European Council on a Commission opinion, and unanimity governs every accession step.",
  "eu040": "The trigger was a 1965 Hallstein Commission package linking CAP financing to own resources and stronger Parliament powers, just as the treaty timetable would have expanded qualified majority voting. France's boycott lasted seven months. The episode illustrates the enduring tension between supranationalism and intergovernmentalism, and the Compromise's veto culture only faded after the Single European Act.",
  "eu041": "The typology of EU legal acts is set out in Article 288 TFEU, which lists regulations, directives, decisions, recommendations and opinions. Regulations enter national legal orders on the date they specify, typically twenty days after publication in the Official Journal, and member states are actually forbidden from transposing them, because national restatement could obscure their EU origin and uniform meaning. EPSO loves testing this against the directive's two-step logic.",
  "eu042": "Transposition deadlines are usually two to three years and are written into each directive's final articles. Since the Lisbon Treaty, Article 260(3) TFEU lets the Court impose financial penalties at the first judgment when a state simply fails to notify transposition measures, without waiting for a second round. The Commission tracks compliance through a public transposition scoreboard, and non-communication cases are its most common infringement type.",
  "eu043": "The ordinary legislative procedure, defined in Article 294 TFEU, was called 'codecision' before Lisbon and was introduced by the Maastricht Treaty in 1993. Lisbon extended it to around 85 policy areas, including agriculture and justice matters, making it the default. Note the trap: the European Council (heads of state or government) has no legislative function at all, per Article 15 TEU.",
  "eu044": "Subsidiarity was introduced as a general principle by the Maastricht Treaty and is now in Article 5(3) TEU, applying only where the EU does not have exclusive competence. Protocol No 2 to the treaties operationalises it, giving national parliaments eight weeks to issue reasoned opinions on draft legislation. Every Commission proposal must include a subsidiarity justification, and the CJEU can review compliance, though it has never annulled an act on this ground.",
  "eu045": "In Costa v ENEL an Italian lawyer contested a tiny electricity bill after Italy nationalised its power sector, and the Court held that states had permanently limited their sovereign rights by creating the Community. Primacy appears nowhere in the treaties; only Declaration 17 annexed to Lisbon recalls the case law. For the CJEU it prevails even over national constitutions (Internationale Handelsgesellschaft, 1970), though Germany's constitutional court contests that outer limit.",
  "eu046": "Van Gend en Loos concerned a Dutch transport company charged a higher customs duty on urea-formaldehyde imports, contrary to the standstill clause of old Article 12 EEC. The Court famously declared that the Community constitutes 'a new legal order of international law' whose subjects include individuals, not just states. Memorise the pairing: Van Gend en Loos 1963 direct effect, Costa v ENEL 1964 primacy — EPSO frequently swaps the two.",
  "eu047": "The infringement procedure has fixed pre-litigation stages: a letter of formal notice, then a reasoned opinion, each usually giving the state two months to reply, before any referral. The Commission has full discretion whether to pursue a case, and individuals cannot force it to act, though complaints often trigger investigations. Under Article 259 TFEU one member state can also sue another directly, but only a handful of such cases have ever been decided.",
  "eu048": "Courts of last instance are obliged to refer questions, while lower courts merely may, subject to the acte clair doctrine from CILFIT (1982), which excuses referral when the answer is obvious. Preliminary rulings are the CJEU's biggest workload and produced landmark cases like Van Gend en Loos and Costa, both of which arrived this way. Only the Court of Justice, never national courts, may declare an EU act invalid (Foto-Frost, 1987).",
  "eu049": "The European Citizens' Initiative was created by the Lisbon Treaty (Article 11(4) TEU) and became operational in April 2012; a 2019 regulation simplified its rules. Organisers need a committee of at least seven citizens from seven different member states and have twelve months to collect signatures. The Commission must respond and hold a hearing but is not obliged to legislate — successful initiatives include Right2Water and End the Cage Age.",
  "eu050": "Article 2 TEU also mentions rights of persons belonging to minorities, and describes a society marked by pluralism, non-discrimination, tolerance, justice, solidarity and equality between women and men. These values are enforceable: Article 49 TEU makes respecting them a condition for applying to join, echoing the political Copenhagen criteria of 1993. The CJEU has increasingly used Article 2 with Article 19 TEU in judicial-independence cases against Poland.",
  "eu051": "Article 7 has two arms: a preventive arm where four-fifths of the Council determines a clear risk of a serious breach, and a sanctioning arm requiring a unanimous European Council determination, excluding the state concerned. The preventive arm was triggered against Poland by the Commission in 2017 (closed 2024) and against Hungary by Parliament in 2018. The unanimity hurdle is why sanctions have never been imposed — two accused states can shield each other.",
  "eu052": "Since the Lisbon Treaty, qualified majority voting is the Council's default rule under Article 16(3) TEU, defined since 2014 as 55 percent of member states representing 65 percent of the EU population. Other unanimity areas worth memorising include social security harmonisation, most of family law, treaty revision, and admitting new members. Even in CFSP, constructive abstention lets a state abstain without blocking a decision.",
  "eu053": "The five exclusive competences are listed exhaustively in Article 3 TFEU; a sixth situation covers concluding international agreements in defined cases (the codified ERTA doctrine). Note the precision traps: monetary policy is exclusive only for euro-area states, and conservation of marine biological resources is exclusive although fisheries policy more broadly is shared. In exclusive areas member states may legislate only if empowered by the EU.",
  "eu054": "Article 4 TFEU lists the shared competences, including internal market, environment, agriculture, transport, energy, consumer protection, cohesion, and the area of freedom, security and justice. The key mechanism is pre-emption: once the EU legislates, member states lose the ability to act in that field, though environment and consumer protection allow stricter national measures. Article 6 TFEU lists the supporting competences where EU harmonisation of national law is expressly excluded.",
  "eu055": "Trilogues emerged in the 1990s as informal practice and now settle roughly 90 percent of legislation at first reading, drastically shortening the formal three-reading procedure. Parliament is typically represented by the rapporteur and shadow rapporteurs, the Council by the rotating presidency, with the Commission as honest broker. Their opacity drew criticism, and after Ombudsman inquiries and the De Capitani case (General Court, 2018), access to four-column negotiating documents has improved.",
  "eu056": "The scope rule comes from Article 51(1) of the Charter, and the CJEU read 'implementing Union law' broadly in Åkerberg Fransson (2013) to cover any national action within the scope of EU law. Distinguish the Charter, an EU instrument enforced by the CJEU in Luxembourg, from the ECHR, a Council of Europe treaty enforced in Strasbourg; Article 52(3) requires corresponding Charter rights to have at least the ECHR's meaning.",
  "eu057": "After the Lisbon Treaty renamed it from 'Court of First Instance' in 2009, the General Court was expanded in stages to fifty-four judges — two per member state — completed in 2019, partly to absorb the abolished Civil Service Tribunal's staff cases. Unlike the Court of Justice, it sits without permanent Advocates General. Since a 2024 reform it also handles certain preliminary rulings in specific areas like customs, excise and air passenger compensation.",
  "eu058": "Decisions became more versatile after Lisbon: those without addressees are used widely in CFSP, where regulations and directives are excluded. Addressed decisions are the workhorse of competition and state aid enforcement — Commission fines on cartels or orders to recover illegal aid take this form. In Grad v Finanzamt Traunstein (1970) the Court held that decisions, like treaty articles, can produce direct effect.",
  "eu059": "This Early Warning System sits in Protocol No 2: each national parliament holds two votes (one per chamber in bicameral systems), and objections must arrive within eight weeks. The yellow-card threshold is one third of all votes, lowered to one quarter for justice and home affairs proposals. It has been triggered only three times — Monti II (2012, withdrawn), the EPPO proposal (2013) and the posted workers revision (2016).",
  "eu060": "Formally Regulation 2020/2092, the conditionality mechanism applies since 1 January 2021; Hungary and Poland challenged it and lost before the full Court in February 2022. The Council imposes measures by qualified majority on a Commission proposal, and in December 2022 it froze around 6.3 billion euros of cohesion funds for Hungary. Distinguish it from Article 7 TEU: conditionality protects the EU budget specifically and needs no unanimity.",
  "eu061": "Jacques Delors' 1985 White Paper and the 1986 Single European Act set the '1992 programme' that turned the four freedoms into a functioning internal market by removing hundreds of non-tariff barriers. Note that free movement of persons originally covered workers only; EU citizenship (Maastricht, 1992) broadened it. Services remain the least integrated freedom, which is why the 2006 Services Directive was so contested.",
  "eu062": "The CAP was launched in 1962 to guarantee food security after wartime shortages, and its early price-support model produced the infamous 'butter mountains' and 'wine lakes' of the 1980s. Successive reforms (MacSharry 1992, Fischler 2003) decoupled payments from production. For 2021-2027 the CAP receives about EUR 387 billion, and each member state now implements it through a national CAP Strategic Plan.",
  "eu063": "Cohesion policy dates from the 1986 Single European Act and was expanded to accompany southern enlargement; it now represents roughly a third of the EU budget (about EUR 392 billion for 2021-2027). Regions are classified as less developed, transition or more developed based on GDP per head versus the EU average, with less developed regions (below 75%) getting the highest co-financing rates.",
  "eu064": "EU competition policy is one of the few areas where the Commission wields direct executive power over companies, subject only to review by the EU courts. Landmark cases include the record Google fines and the blocked Siemens-Alstom merger of 2019. Since 2004, Regulation 1/2003 decentralised antitrust enforcement, letting national competition authorities also apply Articles 101-102 through the European Competition Network.",
  "eu065": "The Green Deal was presented by the von der Leyen Commission in December 2019 as its flagship 'man on the moon moment', spanning energy, industry, transport, farming and biodiversity, with Frans Timmermans as its first czar. The European Climate Law (2021) also created a European Scientific Advisory Board on Climate Change and requires an intermediate 2040 target, for which the Commission proposed a 90% net cut.",
  "eu066": "Presented in July 2021, Fit for 55 comprised about a dozen interlinked laws, including tighter car CO2 standards that effectively end sales of new combustion-engine cars in 2035, the ReFuelEU aviation and FuelEU maritime rules, and the Social Climate Fund. Remember the baseline year is 1990 and the 55% figure refers to net emissions, meaning carbon removals by forests count toward it.",
  "eu067": "The Schengen Agreement was signed in 1985 in the Luxembourg village of Schengen by just five states (France, Germany and Benelux) and was incorporated into the EU legal order only in 1997 with the Amsterdam Treaty. The Schengen Borders Code lets states temporarily reinstate internal checks for serious threats, a clause used extensively since the 2015 migration crisis and COVID-19.",
  "eu068": "Do not confuse the opt-out situations: Denmark has a euro opt-out but is in Schengen, while Ireland is in the euro but outside Schengen. Ireland's position stems from Protocols to the Amsterdam Treaty, which it shared with the UK before Brexit. Cyprus is legally bound to join Schengen eventually, whereas Ireland's opt-out is permanent unless it chooses to opt in.",
  "eu069": "Erasmus is named after the humanist Erasmus of Rotterdam and doubles as an acronym (European Region Action Scheme for the Mobility of University Students). The 2021-2027 programme has a budget of about EUR 26 billion, roughly double its predecessor, and prioritises inclusion, digitalisation and green travel. It also finances the European Universities alliances and DiscoverEU rail passes for 18-year-olds.",
  "eu070": "Horizon Europe is the ninth framework programme (FP9), continuing a series begun in 1984, and is built on three pillars: excellent science, global challenges and industrial competitiveness, and innovative Europe. It introduced five 'missions' (cancer, climate adaptation, oceans, climate-neutral cities, soil) and the European Innovation Council. Non-EU countries can associate, and the UK rejoined as an associated country in 2024.",
  "eu071": "Frontex was created in 2004 and transformed into the European Border and Coast Guard Agency in 2016 after the migration crisis; it is the only EU agency with a uniformed standing corps, planned to reach 10,000 officers by 2027. It is also the EU's largest agency by budget. Member states retain primary responsibility for their own borders, with Frontex providing support and coordination.",
  "eu072": "The DSA, applicable in full since February 2024, keeps the e-Commerce Directive's principle that platforms have no general obligation to monitor content, but adds due-diligence duties scaled by size. Very large online platforms and search engines, those with over 45 million EU users, are supervised directly by the Commission and pay a supervisory fee, and must assess and mitigate systemic risks like disinformation.",
  "eu073": "Banned 'unacceptable risk' practices include social scoring by public authorities, manipulative techniques and untargeted scraping of facial images. The AI Act entered into force in August 2024 with staggered application: prohibitions from February 2025, rules for general-purpose AI models from August 2025, and — after the Digital Omnibus on AI deferred them in July 2026 — high-risk obligations from 2 December 2027 for stand-alone Annex III systems and 2 August 2028 for AI embedded in regulated products. A new AI Office within the Commission supervises general-purpose models.",
  "eu074": "Data protection is a fundamental right under Article 8 of the EU Charter, distinct from privacy under Article 7. The GDPR replaced the 1995 Data Protection Directive and applies extraterritorially to any company targeting people in the EU. Enforcement runs through national data protection authorities using a 'one-stop-shop' lead-authority system, coordinated by the European Data Protection Board; Ireland leads on many US tech firms.",
  "eu075": "The common commercial policy is listed with the customs union, competition rules, euro-area monetary policy and marine conservation among the exclusive competences in Article 3 TFEU. Since Lisbon it also covers foreign direct investment. In Opinion 2/15 on the Singapore agreement, the Court held that certain investment provisions remain shared, which is why some deals are split or ratified as 'mixed' agreements by all national parliaments.",
  "eu076": "The CFP became a fully separate policy in 1983 and its 2013 reform introduced maximum sustainable yield as the guiding standard and a landing obligation banning the discard of unwanted catches. December Council negotiations on quotas are famously long. Fisheries were also a defining issue in the Brexit talks, and the EU shares stock decisions with the UK and Norway for joint waters.",
  "eu077": "Article 168 TFEU limits the EU to complementing national health policies, so the Health Union works through agencies and funding rather than harmonisation. Key building blocks: extended mandates for the ECDC (Stockholm) and EMA (Amsterdam), the HERA preparedness authority created in 2021, the EU4Health programme, and the European Health Data Space regulation (EU) 2025/327, adopted in early 2025, to share health records across borders.",
  "eu078": "The ETS is the Green Deal's main workhorse: the cap declines annually so that covered sectors cut emissions 62% by 2030 versus 2005. Allowances are auctioned or partly allocated free to industries at risk of carbon leakage, though free allocation phases out as CBAM phases in. From 2024 maritime shipping was added, and the Market Stability Reserve absorbs surplus allowances to support the carbon price.",
  "eu079": "During the transitional phase importers only report embedded emissions; the definitive regime starts on 1 January 2026, but under the 2025 CBAM Omnibus the actual purchase of CBAM certificates — priced off the ETS auction price — only begins on 1 February 2027, covering 2026 imports. The covered goods are cement, iron and steel, aluminium, fertilisers, electricity and hydrogen. CBAM is designed to be compatible with WTO rules because it mirrors the carbon cost EU producers already pay, rather than acting as a tariff.",
  "eu080": "The EU Space Programme 2021-2027 (about EUR 14.9 billion) is managed with EUSPA, the EU Agency for the Space Programme in Prague, while ESA, a separate non-EU organisation, handles technical development. Galileo has provided Initial Services since 2016 and its free High Accuracy Service, available since 2023, delivers around 20 cm horizontal accuracy — better than the standard GPS open service; full operational capability has not yet been declared; Copernicus Sentinel satellites supply free Earth-observation data. Newer strands include IRIS2, a planned secure connectivity constellation, and space situational awareness.",
  "eu081": "Memorise the accession sequence: eleven founders adopted the euro in 1999, Greece followed in 2001, then Slovenia (2007), Cyprus and Malta (2008), Slovakia (2009), Estonia (2011), Latvia (2014), Lithuania (2015), Croatia (2023) and Bulgaria (2026). Every member state except Denmark, which holds a treaty opt-out, is legally committed to join once it meets the convergence criteria.",
  "eu082": "A classic EPSO trap is confusing the two launch dates: 1 January 1999 for the euro as an electronic accounting currency in eleven states, and 1 January 2002 for physical notes and coins. Greece squeezed in during 2001, making twelve cash-changeover countries. The timetable itself was fixed by the 1992 Maastricht Treaty, and the ECB was established in June 1998 to prepare the launch.",
  "eu083": "Learn all five tests together: inflation no more than 1.5 percentage points above the three best-performing member states, long-term interest rates within 2 points of the same group, the 3% deficit and 60% debt references, and two years of exchange-rate stability in ERM II. Legal convergence, especially central bank independence, is also assessed. Sweden avoids the euro simply by never joining ERM II.",
  "eu084": "The mandate comes from Article 127 TFEU, and the ECB's 2021 strategy review defined it as a symmetric 2% inflation target over the medium term, replacing the older below-but-close-to-2% formula. Decisions are taken by the Governing Council, the six-member Executive Board plus euro-area national central bank governors. Contrast this single primary objective with the US Federal Reserve's dual mandate covering employment.",
  "eu085": "The MFF is governed by Article 312 TFEU and divides spending into headings such as single market, cohesion, natural resources and administration. Its ceilings bind the annual budgets adopted underneath it. For the exam, note that in July 2025 the Commission proposed the next framework for 2028 to 2034, worth roughly 2 trillion euros, opening years of hard negotiation.",
  "eu086": "In absolute terms the budget now runs at roughly 190 to 200 billion euros a year, temporarily higher while NextGenerationEU money flows. Remember the comparison EPSO likes: national budgets absorb 40 to 50% of GDP, the EU roughly 1% of GNI, yet it funds agriculture, cohesion, research and border management for 450 million people. Also learn the distinction between commitment and payment appropriations.",
  "eu087": "Know the rough revenue shares: GNI-based national contributions supply well over half of revenue and act as the balancing item, customs duties (called traditional own resources) and the VAT-based resource provide most of the rest, and the plastics contribution was added in 2021. Changing own resources requires a unanimous Council decision ratified by every national parliament, which is why new resources such as an ETS share advance so slowly.",
  "eu088": "Useful numbers: NextGenerationEU totals 806.9 billion euros in current prices (750 billion in 2018 prices), of which the Recovery and Resilience Facility accounts for about 723 billion, split between grants of roughly 338 billion and loans of 385 billion. The Commission borrows on capital markets in the EU's name, partly through green bonds, and RRF payments had to be made by end-August 2026.",
  "eu089": "The annual procedure in Article 314 TFEU is a special legislative procedure: the Commission proposes by 1 September, Council adopts a position, Parliament amends, and disagreements go to a 21-day conciliation committee. Since the Lisbon Treaty, Parliament is a fully equal arm of the budgetary authority, having previously had the last word only on non-compulsory expenditure. The budget year matches the calendar year.",
  "eu090": "The Pact dates from 1997 (Amsterdam European Council) and has a preventive arm and a corrective arm, the latter centred on the excessive deficit procedure. Key reform milestones to memorise: the Six-Pack (2011) and Two-Pack (2013) after the euro crisis, the general escape clause activated from 2020 to 2023 during the pandemic, and the fundamental overhaul that entered into force in April 2024.",
  "eu091": "The cycle, created in 2010 after the sovereign debt crisis, runs from the Commission's autumn package (including the Annual Sustainable Growth Survey and the alert mechanism report) through spring country reports to country-specific recommendations endorsed by the European Council and adopted by the Council around June and July. Euro-area states must additionally send draft budgetary plans to the Commission by 15 October each year.",
  "eu092": "The ESM is based on an intergovernmental treaty among euro-area states, outside the EU treaties, with subscribed capital of about 700 billion euros. It replaced the temporary EFSF and financed programmes for Spain's banks, Cyprus and Greece. It is also designated to become the common backstop to the Single Resolution Fund, but that role awaits entry into force of the amended ESM Treaty, still unratified by Italy. Do not confuse it with the ECB, which provides no fiscal bailouts.",
  "eu093": "The Eurogroup gained treaty recognition through Protocol 14 of the Lisbon Treaty but remains informal, so its agreements are formally adopted later in ECOFIN. It usually meets monthly, the evening before ECOFIN, and the ECB President and the economy Commissioner attend. Ireland's Paschal Donohoe held the presidency from 2020 until he resigned in November 2025 to join the World Bank; Greece's finance minister Kyriakos Pierrakakis was elected on 11 December 2025 for a two-and-a-half-year term running from 12 December 2025.",
  "eu094": "Created by the 1957 Treaty of Rome and seated in Luxembourg, the EIB is owned by the 27 member states and borrows on capital markets with a AAA rating, lending tens of billions of euros a year. Together with the European Investment Fund, which supports SMEs, it forms the EIB Group. Distinguish it from the EBRD in London and from the ECB in Frankfurt.",
  "eu095": "A bank counts as significant, and thus falls under direct ECB supervision, mainly when its assets exceed 30 billion euros or 20% of national GDP; roughly 110 banking groups covering the bulk of euro-area banking assets qualify. National supervisors handle the smaller, less significant institutions under ECB oversight. The SSM was the answer to the doom loop between weak banks and weak sovereigns.",
  "eu096": "Distinguish gross contributions from net balances: gross payments track economic size, while net positions subtract EU spending received, making cohesion countries such as Poland the largest net recipients. The UK rebate disappeared with Brexit, but Germany, the Netherlands, Austria, Denmark and Sweden negotiated lump-sum corrections for 2021 to 2027. The 'juste retour' debate over net balances resurfaces at every MFF negotiation.",
  "eu097": "The key instrument is the VAT Directive (2006/112/EC), which also requires reduced rates of at least 5% and, since a 2022 reform, gives states more freedom over reduced and zero rates for listed categories. Remember the institutional point: taxation falls under Article 113 TFEU, so any EU VAT change needs unanimity in the Council with Parliament only consulted.",
  "eu098": "ERM II replaced the original 1979 exchange-rate mechanism when the euro launched in 1999. Bulgaria and Croatia both entered in July 2020, simultaneously joining banking union close cooperation, and each left upon adopting the euro, so Denmark is again the sole participant. Membership must be free of severe tensions and devaluations; the standard band is plus or minus 15% around the euro central rate.",
  "eu099": "The Court of Auditors, in Luxembourg, has one member per member state serving renewable six-year terms; it began work in 1977 and became a full institution with the Maastricht Treaty. Despite its name it has no judicial powers, issuing audit reports and opinions instead. Its French acronym DAS (declaration d'assurance) for the statement of assurance appears frequently in EU budget literature.",
  "eu100": "OLAF was created in 1999 after fraud allegations brought down the Santer Commission. It conducts only administrative investigations, inside the institutions and on EU spending anywhere, and passes recommendations to national prosecutors or the EPPO. The European Public Prosecutor's Office, operational since June 2021 in Luxembourg under Laura Kovesi, brings criminal prosecutions, something OLAF cannot do; only Denmark and Ireland, which hold justice and home affairs opt-outs, remain outside — Poland joined in 2024 and Hungary became the 25th participant in July 2026.",
  "eu101": "The legal gateway is Article 49 TEU: any European state respecting the Article 2 values may apply. The 1995 Madrid European Council added that candidates must also have the administrative and judicial capacity to actually implement the acquis, not just transpose it. The political criteria must be satisfied before negotiations even open, which is why democratic backsliding stalls talks immediately.",
  "eu102": "Remember the sequence: application to the Council, a Commission opinion (the 'avis'), candidate status granted by unanimous European Council decision, negotiations, then an accession treaty ratified by every member state plus the candidate. Unanimity applies at each intermediate step too, creating repeated veto points — Greece long blocked North Macedonia over its name, and Bulgaria later blocked it over history and language disputes.",
  "eu103": "Memorise the candidate timeline: Türkiye 1999, North Macedonia 2005, Montenegro 2010, Serbia 2012, Albania 2014, Ukraine and Moldova June 2022, Bosnia and Herzegovina December 2022, Georgia December 2023. Montenegro is the most advanced, having opened all negotiating chapters and targeting membership around 2028. Kosovo is only a 'potential candidate', a distinct lower status.",
  "eu104": "Technically this is a two-step process: a CFSP Council decision under Article 29 TEU (unanimous) sets the policy, then a regulation under Article 215 TFEU gives it direct effect in EU law, notably for asset freezes. Sanctions must be renewed periodically, usually every six or twelve months, so each rollover is a fresh veto opportunity — Hungary has repeatedly exploited this on Russia measures.",
  "eu105": "Team Europe's collective official development assistance runs to roughly EUR 90 billion a year, well over 40 percent of the global total, with Germany and France among the biggest bilateral donors. Do not confuse development aid (long-term, managed largely by DG INTPA under NDICI-Global Europe) with humanitarian aid (emergency, needs-based, run by DG ECHO) — EPSO questions often test that distinction.",
  "eu106": "The EEAS began operating in January 2011 under the first double-hatted High Representative, Catherine Ashton. Its staff is deliberately hybrid: officials transferred from the Commission and the Council Secretariat plus seconded national diplomats, who must make up at least a third of AD staff. Its headquarters are in Brussels, and it is a sui generis body — neither an institution nor a Commission DG.",
  "eu107": "Distinguish three clauses: Article 42(7) TEU (mutual assistance after armed aggression, EU counterpart to NATO's Article 5), Article 222 TFEU (the solidarity clause for terrorism and disasters), and NATO's Article 5 itself. France chose 42(7) rather than NATO after the November 2015 Paris attacks, receiving bilateral support from partners. The clause binds Ireland, Austria, Malta and Cyprus too, but lets them respond in non-military ways.",
  "eu108": "Know the dates: the TCA was signed on 30 December 2020, applied provisionally from 1 January 2021 and entered into force on 1 May 2021. The 2023 Windsor Framework reworked the Northern Ireland arrangements with 'green lane' goods checks, and a May 2025 EU-UK summit launched a broader 'reset' including a security and defence partnership. The TCA is governed by a Partnership Council and contains rebalancing and review mechanisms.",
  "eu109": "The EEA Agreement has applied since 1994 and covers the four freedoms plus flanking policies, but excludes the customs union, common trade policy, CAP, fisheries and the euro. Enforcement runs through a parallel structure: the EFTA Surveillance Authority and the EFTA Court. Switzerland rejected the EEA in a 1992 referendum; a new package of bilateral framework agreements with Bern was concluded in December 2024 and awaits ratification.",
  "eu110": "The ENP was launched in 2004 after the big-bang enlargement to give neighbours 'everything but institutions'. The Eastern Partnership (Prague, 2009) covers Armenia, Azerbaijan, Belarus (participation suspended), Georgia, Moldova and Ukraine; the Union for the Mediterranean (Paris, 2008) succeeded the 1995 Barcelona Process and has over 40 members. Note that three EaP countries have since jumped tracks into the enlargement process proper.",
  "eu111": "The EPF, created in 2021, sits off-budget because Article 41(2) TEU bars the EU budget from financing operations with military implications. Its ceiling was raised repeatedly to around EUR 17 billion, including a dedicated Ukraine Assistance Fund, and it also finances support to African-led peace operations. EUMAM Ukraine, launched in November 2022, has trained tens of thousands of Ukrainian soldiers on EU territory.",
  "eu112": "The language regime rests on Regulation No 1 of 1958, which the Council can amend only unanimously. The 24 languages use three alphabets — Latin, Greek and Cyrillic (Bulgarian) — and the treaties are equally authentic in all of them. Not every national language qualifies: Luxembourgish and Turkish (an official language in Cyprus) are not EU official languages, a classic trap question.",
  "eu113": "Global Gateway concentrates on five sectors: digital, climate and energy, transport, health, and education and research. The EUR 300 billion headline is mobilised largely through guarantees under EFSD+ and Team Europe co-financing rather than fresh grants. Flagship projects include the Lobito Corridor railway in Africa; the strategy is the EU's geopolitical answer to Beijing's Belt and Road, emphasising standards and debt sustainability.",
  "eu114": "The legal basis is Article 207 TFEU; the Council issues negotiating directives (the 'mandate') and the Commission reports to the Trade Policy Committee and Parliament's INTA committee. After CJEU Opinion 2/15 on the Singapore agreement (2017), which held that portfolio investment and investor-state dispute settlement are shared competences, the EU now often splits deals: an EU-only trade agreement plus a separate mixed investment protection agreement.",
  "eu115": "All three 2022 applications were direct reactions to Russia's full-scale invasion: Ukraine applied within days (28 February), Moldova and Georgia on 3 March. Ukraine and Moldova received candidate status in June 2022 and opened accession negotiations in June 2024, while Georgia lagged behind. Tbilisi's 'foreign agents' law and disputed 2024 elections led the EU to declare the process effectively halted and to curtail high-level contacts.",
  "eu116": "The EU ratified the Paris Agreement in October 2016, helping trigger its rapid entry into force, and submits a single joint Nationally Determined Contribution for all member states. Its targets — at least a 55 percent net emissions cut by 2030 and climate neutrality by 2050 — are legally binding internally through the European Climate Law. The Carbon Border Adjustment Mechanism extends this agenda externally by pricing embedded carbon in imports.",
  "eu117": "PESCO's legal basis is Articles 42(6) and 46 TEU plus Protocol 10, dormant since Lisbon until activated in December 2017 by 25 states. Denmark joined in 2023 after abolishing its defence opt-out, leaving Malta as the sole abstainer. Its 60-plus projects include Military Mobility, notable as the first opened to third countries — the United States, Canada, Norway and later the UK participate.",
  "eu118": "DG ECHO does not implement aid itself: it funds partners such as UN agencies, the Red Cross movement and certified NGOs, with an annual humanitarian budget of roughly EUR 2 billion, often topped up in crises. Through the Union Civil Protection Mechanism it can deploy the rescEU reserve — EU-financed firefighting aircraft, medical evacuation capacity and emergency stockpiles created after the 2017-18 wildfire seasons.",
  "eu119": "Trace the lineage for exams: Yaoundé Conventions (1963), Lomé Conventions (from 1975), Cotonou Agreement (2000), Samoa Agreement (signed in Apia, November 2023). The Samoa Agreement adds three regional protocols for Africa, the Caribbean and the Pacific. A key funding change came earlier: the European Development Fund, historically outside the EU budget, was folded into NDICI-Global Europe in 2021.",
  "eu120": "The framework is Regulation 2019/452, applicable since October 2020, prompted by Chinese acquisitions of strategic firms such as robotics maker Kuka in 2016. The Commission and other member states can issue comments and opinions, but these are not binding on the screening state. Nearly all member states now operate national mechanisms, and a 2024 Commission proposal would tighten the system by making screening mandatory in defined sectors.",
  "eu121": "There are eleven Advocates General; the largest member states (Germany, France, Italy, Spain and Poland) always have one, while the rest rotate. Appointments require a favourable opinion from the Article 255 panel, which vets candidates' suitability. Half the judgeships are renewed every three years, and the judges elect their own President for a renewable three-year term; Koen Lenaerts has presided since 2015.",
  "eu122": "Eight groups formed after the 2024 elections, with the centre-right EPP the largest, followed by S&D and new right-wing formations such as Patriots for Europe; MEPs who join no group sit as non-attached ('non-inscrits'). MEPs sit by political affiliation, not nationality, and the 'quarter of member states' rule prevents purely national parties forming a group.",
  "eu123": "The Parliament's presidency customarily changes mid-term under power-sharing deals between the big groups, making Metsola's full-term tenure notable; she is only the third woman in the post after Simone Veil (the first, in 1979) and Nicole Fontaine. She became President after David Sassoli died in office in January 2022. The President signs adopted legislation and the EU budget into effect.",
  "eu124": "The number of configurations was cut from 22 to 16 at Helsinki in 1999 and from 16 to 9 at Seville in 2002, reaching the current 10 when Lisbon split the General Affairs and External Relations Council in two. The General Affairs Council coordinates the others and prepares European Council meetings. Whatever the configuration, it is legally one single institution, so any configuration can formally adopt any act, which is why 'A points' can be rubber-stamped by unrelated ministers.",
  "eu125": "Parliament currently has around 20 standing committees, and their names are worth recognising in abbreviated form: AFET (foreign affairs), ECON, ENVI, LIBE (civil liberties), BUDG and AGRI among them. Committees appoint a rapporteur to draft Parliament's position on each legislative file, and 'trilogue' negotiations with Council and Commission are conducted on the basis of committee mandates.",
  "eu126": "The independence duty is in Article 17(3) TEU, and Commissioners take a solemn oath before the Court of Justice in Luxembourg. Breaches can lead the Court, on application by the Council or the Commission, to compulsorily retire a Commissioner or strip pension rights (Article 245 TFEU). The Dalli case in 2012 shows individual Commissioners can be forced out over conduct issues.",
  "eu127": "Consensus means the President concludes that no leader objects, so nothing is formally voted; this preserves the body's character as a strategic organ. Note two more rules: when the European Council does vote, its own President and the Commission President do not take part, and Article 15 TEU expressly bars the European Council from exercising legislative functions.",
  "eu128": "Because the euro area now has 21 members (Bulgaria joined on 1 January 2026, after Croatia in 2023), the Governing Council uses a rotation system for voting rights: the five largest economies share four votes and the remaining governors share eleven, rotating monthly. The six-member Executive Board runs day-to-day business and implements the monetary policy the Governing Council decides.",
  "eu129": "The EMA relocation was decided by drawing lots after Amsterdam and Milan tied in the Council vote in 2017, a memorable detail examiners like. The agency coordinates the centralised marketing-authorisation procedure, with the final legal authorisation formally granted by the Commission. Its committee for human medicines is known as the CHMP; it gained prominence approving COVID-19 vaccines.",
  "eu130": "Europol began as a drugs unit in the 1990s and became a full EU agency in 2010; it supports national forces with intelligence analysis and coordination but has no power to arrest anyone. Other agency seats worth learning: EUAA (asylum) in Malta, ENISA in Athens, EBA and ESMA in Paris, EIOPA in Frankfurt, and the EU Agency for Fundamental Rights in Vienna.",
  "eu131": "The EPPO was established under Article 86 TFEU because unanimity could not be reached; 25 member states now participate, with Sweden, Poland and most recently Hungary joining, while only Ireland and Denmark remain outside. Its first European Chief Prosecutor is Romania's Laura Codruța Kövesi. It prosecutes before national courts, and its remit includes fraud, corruption and cross-border VAT fraud above 10 million euros.",
  "eu132": "Eurojust became an agency under a 2018 regulation and hosts the case files of Joint Investigation Teams; since 2022 it can also preserve evidence of core international crimes, a mandate expanded after Russia's invasion of Ukraine. Distinguish three actors: Europol (police intelligence), Eurojust (judicial coordination) and the EPPO (actual prosecution of EU-budget crimes).",
  "eu133": "The permanent President's tasks under Article 15 TEU include driving forward the European Council's work, ensuring continuity, reporting to Parliament after each summit, and representing the EU externally at head-of-state level on foreign and security policy, without prejudice to the High Representative. The President cannot hold any national office simultaneously, which is why holders resign national posts first.",
  "eu134": "There are roughly 33 DGs plus services such as the Legal Service, the Secretariat-General and OLAF; each DG answers to a Commissioner, though portfolios and DGs do not always map one-to-one. The Commission employs about 32,000 staff, the largest EU administration, and its working headquarters is the Berlaymont building in Brussels. Executive agencies like REA implement programmes on its behalf.",
  "eu135": "Article 16(3) TEU makes QMV the default voting rule in the Council; unanimity survives in taxation, most of the CFSP, enlargement, own resources and family law, where any one government can veto. Abstention does not block unanimity, a frequently tested nuance. 'Passerelle' clauses would allow moving areas from unanimity to QMV, but activating them itself requires unanimity.",
  "eu136": "In 2019 the EPP's lead candidate Manfred Weber was rejected by the European Council, which nominated von der Leyen, who had not campaigned at all; in 2024 von der Leyen ran as EPP Spitzenkandidatin and got the job, partially reviving the idea. The concept rests on Lisbon's phrase that election results be 'taken into account', not on any binding rule.",
  "eu137": "The CoR has 329 members who must hold a regional or local electoral mandate, such as mayors or regional presidents. Beyond mandatory consultation, Lisbon gave it the right to challenge legislative acts before the Court of Justice for breach of subsidiarity. If it is not consulted where the treaties require, the resulting act can be annulled for procedural violation.",
  "eu138": "The Court of Auditors' members are appointed by the Council acting by qualified majority, and Parliament's opinion, though required, is not binding — the Council has occasionally appointed candidates Parliament rejected. Members elect their own President for three years; each member is assigned to one of five audit chambers. Like Commissioners, they may be compulsorily retired by the Court of Justice for misconduct.",
  "eu139": "The Eurogroup gained treaty recognition only in a Lisbon protocol; it meets the evening before ECOFIN and takes no legally binding decisions, yet it shaped the euro-crisis bailouts. Donohoe is its third permanent-style president after Jean-Claude Juncker and Jeroen Dijsselbloem (Mário Centeno also served). The ECB President and the economy Commissioner attend its meetings as participants.",
  "eu140": "The EU has 24 official languages, and every regulation must be published in all of them in the Official Journal, each version equally authentic. The Commission's DG Translation and DG Interpretation (formerly SCIC) are the largest services; Parliament's plenary offers interpretation across all 24 languages, implying over 550 possible language combinations. Irish gained full status only in 2022.",
  "eu141": "The June 1955 conference of the six foreign ministers deliberately shifted strategy from failed political and military integration to economics, the so-called 'relance europeenne'. The UK sent only a mid-level observer to the Spaak Committee and soon withdrew, one of history's noted missed opportunities. The Spaak Report of 1956 became the direct basis for the Val Duchesse negotiations that produced Rome.",
  "eu142": "The EDC treaty had been signed by all six ECSC states in 1952, on a French initiative, the Pleven Plan, designed to make German rearmament acceptable. Ironically it was France's own parliament that killed it in August 1954, with Gaullists and Communists voting together. West Germany instead rearmed within NATO via the Western European Union, and defence returned to EU treaties only with Maastricht.",
  "eu143": "Greenland's February 1982 referendum was driven mainly by anger over the common fisheries policy giving other members access to its waters. It negotiated Overseas Country and Territory status, keeping ties to the EU through Denmark plus a fisheries agreement. The three-year gap between the vote and exit in 1985 is often cited as a comparator for how long withdrawal negotiations take.",
  "eu144": "Formally the 'Treaty establishing a Single Council and a Single Commission', it was signed in Brussels in 1965 and applied from 1 July 1967. The Parliamentary Assembly and Court of Justice had already been shared by all three Communities since 1958. The Merger Treaty was later repealed by the Amsterdam Treaty, its content absorbed into the main treaties.",
  "eu145": "De Gaulle's January 1963 press-conference veto stunned partners because negotiations were well advanced; he doubted Britain's European commitment and feared it would be an American 'Trojan horse'. The same month he signed the Elysee friendship treaty with Adenauer's Germany. His second 'non' came in November 1967 against Harold Wilson's Labour government's application.",
  "eu146": "The Stockholm Convention founders were the 'outer seven': the UK, Austria, Denmark, Norway, Portugal, Sweden and Switzerland, contrasted with the EEC's 'inner six'. EFTA involved no supranational institutions, no common external tariff and no agriculture coverage. Since 1994 three EFTA states, all except Switzerland, participate in the single market through the European Economic Area agreement.",
  "eu147": "This fourth enlargement, effective 1 January 1995, brought in three wealthy, formerly neutral states whose accession became politically possible after the Cold War ended. All three had first joined the new European Economic Area in 1994. Their entry raised membership to fifteen, the number that then undertook the 2004 big-bang enlargement, and added Swedish and Finnish as official languages.",
  "eu148": "Delivered at the University of Zurich on 19 September 1946, the speech proposed that France and Germany, not Britain, take the lead. Churchill went on to chair the 1948 Hague Congress, which led to the Council of Europe in 1949, a body he championed. He is counted among the EU's official 'founding fathers' despite Britain's later ambivalence.",
  "eu149": "Monnet, a cognac merchant's son who never held elected office, headed France's postwar Modernisation Plan before proposing the coal-steel pool to Schuman. After leaving the High Authority he founded the Action Committee for the United States of Europe to lobby governments. His functionalist, step-by-step approach is contrasted in exams with Spinelli's federalist, constitution-first method.",
  "eu150": "Spinelli spent sixteen years in fascist prisons and confinement, co-writing the Manifesto with Ernesto Rossi on cigarette papers smuggled off Ventotene island. He later served as a European Commissioner and then an MEP, where he led the 'Crocodile Club' that produced the 1984 draft treaty. Though never ratified, that draft strongly influenced the Single European Act and Maastricht.",
  "eu151": "Laeken marked a new answer to the 'Nice leftovers': instead of another closed intergovernmental conference, a broad Convention of national and European parliamentarians, government representatives and Commission members would deliberate in public. It ran from 2002 to 2003 with 105 members. The same convention method is now enshrined in Article 48 TEU as the default route for treaty revision.",
  "eu152": "Ireland must hold referendums whenever a treaty alters its constitution, following the Supreme Court's 1987 Crotty judgment, which is why Irish votes recur in EU history. The 2008 Lisbon rejection was answered with guarantees on taxation, neutrality and abortion, plus a promise to keep one Commissioner per member state, a concession that still shapes the Commission's size today.",
  "eu153": "The flag was designed by Arsene Heitz and Paul Levy for the Council of Europe, which is why it also flies for that separate 46-member organisation. Twelve was chosen as a symbol of perfection and unity, echoing months of the year and hours on a clock. Lisbon dropped the flag from treaty text, but sixteen member states affirmed it in a declaration.",
  "eu154": "The Council of Europe adopted the anthem in 1972, and the European Communities followed in 1985 alongside the flag, both on the Adonnino Committee's recommendations for a 'People's Europe'. The official arrangement was prepared by conductor Herbert von Karajan. Friedrich Schiller wrote the 1785 poem Beethoven set to music, but the EU uses only the instrumental version.",
  "eu155": "The motto emerged from a 2000 competition among European school pupils and was cited in the abandoned Constitutional Treaty, though Lisbon dropped the symbols articles. It captures the idea that integration does not erase national identities, echoed legally in Article 4(2) TEU on respect for national identity. Distinguish it from 'ever closer union among the peoples of Europe' in the TEU preamble.",
  "eu156": "The prize was accepted in Oslo jointly by the three presidents: Van Rompuy for the European Council, Barroso for the Commission and Schulz for the Parliament, itself a lesson in EU polycentrism. The award came pointedly during the eurozone debt crisis, honouring the reconciliation of France and Germany, Spain, Portugal and Greece's democratic transitions, and eastern enlargement.",
  "eu157": "The EMS was driven by Chancellor Helmut Schmidt and President Valery Giscard d'Estaing after the collapse of Bretton Woods and the failed currency 'snake'. Its Exchange Rate Mechanism kept currencies within fluctuation bands around central rates. The 1992-93 ERM crisis, when sterling and the lira were forced out on Black Wednesday, is a classic exam reference point.",
  "eu158": "The report was commissioned by the 1969 Hague summit, the same meeting that unblocked British accession, under the triptych 'completion, deepening, enlargement'. Its three-stage design, fixed exchange rates and eventual single monetary policy, closely prefigured the Delors Report and Maastricht's EMU stages. The collapse of Bretton Woods and the 1973 oil shock buried the original 1980 target.",
  "eu159": "Signed by 25 of the then 27 member states in March 2012, the TSCG obliges parties to enshrine a balanced-budget 'golden rule' in national law, preferably at constitutional level. It shows how eurozone crisis governance sometimes bypassed the treaties, like the ESM treaty the same year. Its drafters intended to fold it into EU law within five years, which has still not happened.",
  "eu160": "Denmark originally held four Edinburgh opt-outs: defence, the euro, justice and home affairs, and EU citizenship (the last rendered moot by Amsterdam). The June 2022 vote was Denmark's first-ever removal of an opt-out; a 2015 referendum on converting the justice opt-out had failed. Denmark has since joined EU defence initiatives including PESCO and the European Defence Agency.",
  "eu161": "Annulment actions must be brought within two months of publication or notification, and the grounds are lack of competence, infringement of essential procedural requirements, infringement of the treaties, and misuse of powers. Lisbon eased standing slightly: for 'regulatory acts' not entailing implementing measures, individuals need only show direct concern. Pair Article 263 with Article 265 TFEU, the action for failure to act, its procedural mirror image.",
  "eu162": "Enhanced cooperation is governed by Article 20 TEU and Articles 326 to 334 TFEU; authorisation comes from the Council with Parliament's consent, and only participating states vote on the resulting acts. Acts adopted this way bind only participants and are not part of the acquis new members must accept. The unitary patent, operational since June 2023 with its Unified Patent Court, is the most prominent recent use.",
  "eu163": "The general passerelle is Article 48(7) TEU and can also switch a special legislative procedure to the ordinary one; specific passerelles exist for areas like family law, social policy, environment and the MFF. Any single national parliament can block the general passerelle within six months of notification. Defence and military matters are expressly excluded, and the Commission has repeatedly urged using passerelles for taxation and foreign policy sanctions, without success.",
  "eu164": "A mnemonic that works: delegated acts change the rulebook's non-essential content, implementing acts apply it uniformly. Delegated acts follow the Lisbon replacement of the old 'regulatory procedure with scrutiny'; either co-legislator can object, usually within two months, or revoke the delegation entirely. Essential elements of a policy can never be delegated — the Court confirmed the boundary in cases like Commission v Parliament and Council (biocides, 2014).",
  "eu165": "Comitology is now governed by Regulation 182/2011, which reduced the old maze to two procedures: advisory (opinion not binding) and examination (a qualified-majority negative opinion blocks adoption). If a committee gives no opinion, the Commission can often still adopt the measure, which is how contested pesticide approvals like glyphosate proceeded. Around 200 to 300 committees of national officials handle everything from customs codes to food safety authorisations.",
  "eu166": "The order of the six titles — Dignity, Freedoms, Equality, Solidarity, Citizens' Rights, Justice — is itself a memorable exam item, with a seventh title of general provisions on interpretation and scope. The Charter innovates beyond the ECHR with modern rights like data protection (Article 8), the right to good administration (Article 41) and bioethics guarantees. Protocol 30 gives Poland a limited interpretive opt-out; the UK's lapsed with Brexit.",
  "eu167": "Article 6(2) TEU makes accession a legal obligation, but in Opinion 2/13 of December 2014 the CJEU found the 2013 draft agreement incompatible on grounds including the autonomy of EU law, mutual trust between member states, and CFSP jurisdiction. Renegotiations produced a revised draft in March 2023, yet accession remains unfinished, partly over the unresolved CFSP jurisdiction gap. Meanwhile Article 6(3) TEU treats ECHR rights as general principles of EU law.",
  "eu168": "The acquis is currently organised into 35 negotiating chapters, grouped since 2020 into six thematic clusters, with the 'fundamentals' cluster (judiciary, rule of law, public procurement) opened first and closed last. Transitional periods can delay application of parts of the acquis but permanent opt-outs are not offered to newcomers. The concept also matters in reverse: Brexit negotiations had to disentangle the UK from the entire acquis.",
  "eu169": "Consent, called the 'assent procedure' when the Single European Act introduced it in 1987, also covers withdrawal agreements under Article 50 TEU, Article 7 determinations, and association agreements. Parliament vetoed ACTA in July 2012 by 478 votes to 39, its most famous rejection of an international agreement. Most agreements are voted under Article 218(6) TFEU; the annual budget instead has its own special procedure with real amendment power.",
  "eu170": "The foundational case is Roquette Frères v Council (1980), the 'isoglucose' judgment, where the Court annulled a regulation because the Council had not waited for Parliament's opinion. The Council must also re-consult Parliament if it substantially amends the proposal. Consultation was the original 1957 procedure when Parliament was a mere appointed Assembly, and its survival in taxation and competition marks the areas governments guard most jealously.",
  "eu171": "Recommendations and opinions are 'soft law', yet in Grimaldi (1989) the CJEU held that national courts must take recommendations into account when they clarify binding provisions. The Commission uses recommendations to steer politically sensitive fields like the European Semester's country-specific recommendations. Watch for hybrid instruments too: interinstitutional agreements, guidelines and communications bind their author through legitimate expectations even though Article 288 does not list them.",
  "eu172": "Legal personality is conferred by Article 47 TEU, and Lisbon simultaneously abolished the old three-pillar structure, merging the Community into the Union. Practical consequences include the EU's own seat in bodies such as the WTO and FAO, its accession to conventions like the Istanbul Convention (2023), and the ability to be sued internationally. International agreements the EU concludes bind its institutions and member states and rank below the treaties but above secondary legislation.",
  "eu173": "The full case name is Rewe-Zentral AG v Bundesmonopolverwaltung für Branntwein (1979): Germany blocked the French blackcurrant liqueur for having too little alcohol. The judgment created the 'mandatory requirements' doctrine allowing non-discriminatory national rules for consumer protection or public health, provided they are proportionate. It builds on the Dassonville formula (1974) defining measures of equivalent effect, later trimmed by Keck (1993) for mere selling arrangements.",
  "eu174": "Francovich concerned Directive 80/987 on protecting employees in employer insolvency, which Italy had ignored despite a prior infringement judgment. Brasserie du Pêcheur and Factortame III (1996) extended liability to any state breach, including by legislatures, and fixed the three conditions: the rule confers rights on individuals, the breach is sufficiently serious, and there is a direct causal link. Köbler (2003) even extended liability to breaches by supreme courts.",
  "eu175": "Jean-Marc Bosman was a Belgian midfielder whose club, RFC Liège, demanded a fee after his contract expired, blocking his move to Dunkerque; the Court applied what is now Article 45 TFEU. The ruling transformed football's labour market, enabling free transfers. It also shows that free movement provisions bind private regulatory bodies like UEFA, not just states — horizontal application, earlier signalled in Walrave and Koch (1974).",
  "eu176": "The test has been progressively softened: 'unconditional' now mainly means no discretion is left to states in implementation. Direct effect extends beyond treaty articles to regulations, decisions, and provisions of international agreements the EU concludes. Distinguish vertical from horizontal carefully — treaty articles can have both (Defrenne v Sabena, 1976, on equal pay), whereas directives are confined to vertical effect against the state.",
  "eu177": "Vertical effect requires the transposition deadline to have expired, and the Court defines 'the state' broadly (Foster v British Gas, 1990) to include any body providing a public service under state control with special powers. The rule against horizontal effect comes from Marshall (1986) and was confirmed in Faccini Dori (1994). The duty of consistent interpretation, from Von Colson (1984) and Marleasing (1990), often achieves indirectly what horizontal direct effect would.",
  "eu178": "The orange card requires a simple majority of the votes allocated to national parliaments (each parliament has two). The proposal then goes forward only with a reasoned Commission justification, and either 55 percent of Council members or a majority of votes cast in Parliament can terminate it at first reading. Unlike the yellow card, the orange card exists only for the ordinary legislative procedure and has never once been triggered.",
  "eu179": "Regulation No 1 of 1958, the very first act of the Community, fixed the language regime; today there are 24 official languages, Irish having gained full working status in 2022. The leading case on multilingual interpretation is CILFIT (1982), which cited language divergence as a reason interpretation is rarely obvious. Where versions diverge, the Court resolves conflicts teleologically, by purpose and scheme, not by a majority of languages.",
  "eu180": "Proportionality appears in Article 5(4) TEU and classically involves three limbs: suitability, necessity, and no excessive burden relative to the aim. It cuts both ways, constraining EU legislation (Protocol No 2 covers it alongside subsidiarity) and national measures derogating from the four freedoms. It entered EU law as a general principle drawn from German law in Internationale Handelsgesellschaft (1970), and Digital Rights Ireland (2014) used it to annul the Data Retention Directive.",
  "eu181": "A useful memory hook: Pillar 1 is fully EU-financed and annual (direct income payments plus market intervention), while Pillar 2 is multiannual, co-financed and tailored to national rural development needs. The 2023-2027 CAP added 'eco-schemes' inside Pillar 1, rewarding climate- and environment-friendly practices, and requires 'conditionality', meaning payments depend on meeting environmental and animal-welfare standards.",
  "eu182": "REACH stands for Registration, Evaluation, Authorisation and restriction of CHemicals and dates from 2006, replacing dozens of older directives. It reversed the burden of proof: industry, not authorities, must demonstrate chemical safety. Substances of very high concern, such as carcinogens and endocrine disruptors, go on a candidate list and may require authorisation. ECHA in Helsinki also manages the CLP classification and labelling system.",
  "eu183": "Track the target's evolution for exams: 20% by 2020 (achieved), then 32% under RED II, raised to 42.5% by RED III after Russia's invasion of Ukraine made energy independence urgent. REPowerEU (May 2022) drove the increase, alongside faster permitting rules for wind and solar. The share was around 24-25% in the mid-2020s, so the target implies nearly doubling renewables within a decade.",
  "eu184": "Farm to Fork, published in May 2020 alongside the Biodiversity Strategy, set headline 2030 targets: 50% cuts in pesticide use and risk, at least 20% less fertiliser use, 50% less antimicrobial sales for farm animals, and 25% of farmland organic. Note the political backlash: after the 2024 farmer protests the Commission withdrew its pesticide regulation proposal (SUR), so several targets remain aspirational rather than legislated.",
  "eu185": "The Pact's key legal instruments include a screening regulation, a reformed Eurodac biometric database, an Asylum and Migration Management Regulation replacing parts of the Dublin system, and a crisis regulation. The solidarity pool foresees around 30,000 relocations a year, with a notional EUR 20,000 contribution per person not relocated. Hungary and Poland voted against, illustrating how divisive migration remains despite qualified-majority adoption.",
  "eu186": "SIS dates from 1995 as the compensating measure for abolishing internal borders, and its current generation added palm prints, DNA for missing persons and preventive alerts on vulnerable children. Distinguish the alphabet soup: SIS covers alerts, VIS covers visa applications, Eurodac stores asylum fingerprints, EES logs entries and exits, and ETIAS, whose start date is still to be announced and now expected in 2027, will pre-screen visa-exempt travellers.",
  "eu187": "'Roam like at home' came after a decade of stepwise price caps that the Parliament pushed hard for, a classic example of the EP driving consumer-friendly legislation. Operators may apply fair-use limits on data and can request a derogation if losses are proven. The rules cover the EEA, so Iceland, Liechtenstein and Norway are included, but the UK dropped out after Brexit.",
  "eu188": "The EHIC rests on the social-security coordination regulations (883/2004), which rest on the free movement of persons; they coordinate but never harmonise national systems. Costs are billed back to your home insurer. It applies in the EU, EEA countries and Switzerland, and post-Brexit the UK issues a similar GHIC. If local patients pay co-payments, EHIC holders pay them too, hence 'not travel insurance'.",
  "eu189": "The Pillar's 20 principles are grouped in three chapters: equal opportunities and labour-market access, fair working conditions, and social protection and inclusion. It is a proclamation, not a treaty, but it has spawned binding legislation such as the Work-Life Balance Directive, the Transparent and Predictable Working Conditions Directive and the Minimum Wage Directive. The 2021 Porto Summit endorsed its 2030 headline targets.",
  "eu190": "The legal base is Article 153 TFEU, which explicitly excludes 'pay' from EU harmonisation, so the directive was carefully drafted to set procedures, not wage levels; Denmark, backed by Sweden, challenged it, and in Case C-19/23 (11 November 2025) the Court partially annulled Article 5(2) and part of Article 5(3) as direct interference with pay, while upholding the rest of the directive. It references indicative adequacy benchmarks like 60% of the median wage, and states below 80% bargaining coverage must adopt action plans.",
  "eu191": "The DMA's quantitative thresholds are worth knowing: roughly EUR 7.5 billion European turnover or EUR 75 billion market capitalisation, plus 45 million monthly EU end users. Obligations include allowing sideloading and interoperability, and prohibitions include self-preferencing and combining personal data across services without consent. Unlike antitrust cases that take years, DMA duties apply ex ante, automatically, once a gatekeeper is designated.",
  "eu192": "NIS2 replaced the 2016 NIS Directive, the EU's first cybersecurity law, and had to be transposed by October 2024. It distinguishes 'essential' from 'important' entities, imposes management-level accountability including possible bans on executives, and sets a 24-hour early-warning deadline for significant incidents. It sits in a wider framework with the Cyber Resilience Act for products and DORA for the financial sector.",
  "eu193": "The EU's share of global chip production had fallen to under 10%, and the 2021 shortage that halted car plants made semiconductors a strategic priority. The 2023 Act mobilises EUR 43 billion of public and private investment across three pillars: research capacity, incentives for 'first-of-a-kind' mega-fabs, and a crisis-response toolbox. It parallels the US CHIPS Act in the global subsidy race.",
  "eu194": "The Act, adopted in 2024 as part of the Green Deal Industrial Plan, lists 34 critical and 17 strategic raw materials, including lithium, cobalt, rare earths and gallium, essential for batteries, wind turbines and chips. Strategic projects get fast-track permitting. The dependency figure to remember is China, which dominates rare-earth processing, motivating the 65% single-supplier ceiling and partnerships with countries like Canada and Chile.",
  "eu195": "The JTF has about EUR 17.5 billion and is the first pillar of the wider Just Transition Mechanism, which also includes an InvestEU scheme and a public-sector loan facility with the EIB. Access requires territorial just transition plans identifying affected areas, classic examples being Polish Silesia and Greek lignite regions. It is delivered under cohesion-policy rules, showing how climate and regional policy intertwine.",
  "eu196": "Natura 2000 rests on the Birds Directive (1979, the EU's oldest nature law) and the Habitats Directive (1992), which created Special Protection Areas and Special Areas of Conservation respectively. Sites are not wilderness reserves; farming and forestry continue, but plans and projects need an 'appropriate assessment' if they could significantly affect a site, a test the Court of Justice interprets strictly.",
  "eu197": "The law nearly died twice: the EPP campaigned against it in Parliament in 2023, and in 2024 the Council only adopted it when Austria's climate minister voted in favour against her chancellor's wishes. Targets rise from 20% by 2030 to cover all ecosystems needing restoration by 2050, with specific obligations on pollinators, drained peatlands, free-flowing rivers and urban green space.",
  "eu198": "Directive 2005/36/EC distinguishes automatic recognition, based on harmonised minimum training, from the 'general system', where authorities compare qualifications and may impose compensation measures like an aptitude test. The European Professional Card exists for a few professions such as nurses and physiotherapists. Lawyers follow separate directives, and note that qualification recognition is distinct from the CJEU's diploma case law on free movement.",
  "eu199": "Created in 2007, the ERC is science-driven: its Scientific Council, not the Commission, sets strategy, and sole evaluation criterion is excellence, with no policy relevance test and no consortium requirement, unlike most Horizon funding. Grant types to know: Starting, Consolidator, Advanced and Synergy, worth roughly EUR 1.5 to 2.5 million or more. Several ERC grantees have gone on to win Nobel Prizes.",
  "eu200": "Created in 2001 and headquartered around the Emergency Response Coordination Centre (ERCC) in Brussels, the Mechanism includes all member states plus about ten participating states such as Norway, Türkiye and Ukraine. It has been activated for COVID-19 repatriations, the Ukraine war (its largest operation ever) and Mediterranean wildfires. rescEU assets are EU-financed up to 100% but hosted and operated by member states.",
  "eu201": "Learn the six non-euro states as a set: Czechia, Denmark, Hungary, Poland, Romania and Sweden. Only Denmark holds a legal opt-out; the other five are treaty-bound to join eventually but control the timing, since ERM II entry is voluntary. Sweden even rejected the euro in a 2003 referendum yet remains formally obliged. Romania is the only one publicly targeting adoption.",
  "eu202": "The three rates form a corridor: the marginal lending rate on top, the main refinancing rate in the middle, and the deposit facility rate at the bottom. Since the post-2022 tightening cycle, abundant liquidity has made the deposit facility rate the operative benchmark, and in 2024 the ECB narrowed the spread between it and the refinancing rate to 15 basis points. EURIBOR, by contrast, is a market-determined interbank rate.",
  "eu203": "A digital euro would be a retail central bank digital currency: a direct claim on the ECB, distributed through supervised intermediaries, with individual holding limits to protect bank deposits and an offline mode for privacy. The Commission proposed the enabling legislation in June 2023, and the ECB has been in a preparation phase since November 2023; no launch can occur before the regulation is adopted.",
  "eu204": "The SRB sits in Brussels and administers the Single Resolution Fund, prefunded by bank levies to about 1% of covered deposits, roughly 80 billion euros. Resolution applies the bail-in principle: shareholders and creditors absorb losses before any public money, reversing the taxpayer-funded bailouts of 2008 to 2012. Euro-area states agreed in 2020 to give the Fund a common backstop — an ESM credit line roughly doubling its firepower — but the amended ESM Treaty that creates it has still not entered into force, because Italy alone has not ratified it, so the backstop is not yet available.",
  "eu205": "EDIS was proposed by the Commission back in 2015 but stalled over northern states' fear of mutualising legacy risks in southern banks. Meanwhile, harmonised national schemes already guarantee deposits up to 100,000 euros per depositor per bank under the Deposit Guarantee Schemes Directive. Remember the pillar order for the exam: SSM supervision (2014), SRM resolution (2016), deposit insurance still missing.",
  "eu206": "CMU launched in 2015 under the Juncker Commission and was relaunched as the Savings and Investments Union in a March 2025 strategy, after the 2024 Letta and Draghi reports warned that fragmented capital markets cripple European competitiveness and let hundreds of billions in EU savings flow abroad, largely to the United States, each year. Watch the debate on giving ESMA direct supervisory powers.",
  "eu207": "Eurostat is a directorate-general of the Commission, not an agency, and works through the European Statistical System with national statistical institutes. Its credibility matters constitutionally: Greece's misreported deficit figures before 2010 triggered reforms giving Eurostat audit-like verification powers over national fiscal data. It also produces the harmonised index of consumer prices (HICP), the inflation measure the ECB targets.",
  "eu208": "The legal basis is Article 126 TFEU with reference values fixed in Protocol 12. The procedure escalates from a Commission report to a Council decision that an excessive deficit exists, recommendations with deadlines, and ultimately possible fines for euro-area states, never yet imposed. After the pandemic escape clause ended, the Commission opened procedures against several states including France and Italy in 2024.",
  "eu209": "The MIP came in with the 2011 Six-Pack to fix a blind spot: Ireland and Spain met the fiscal rules before 2008 while private credit and housing bubbles built up. A scoreboard of around fourteen indicators, such as current-account balances and house-price growth, triggers in-depth reviews. The corrective excessive imbalance procedure, with potential fines, has never been activated.",
  "eu210": "Introduced on 1 January 2021, this was the first genuinely new own resource since 1988 and yields roughly 6 to 7 billion euros a year. It is a contribution paid by national treasuries, calculated on non-recycled plastic packaging waste, not an EU tax on producers or citizens, and lower-income member states receive lump-sum reductions. It doubles as an environmental incentive supporting circular-economy goals.",
  "eu211": "The balance rule sits in Article 310 TFEU: revenue and expenditure must be in equilibrium, so the Union traditionally cannot deficit-finance itself. NextGenerationEU circumvented this via the Own Resources Decision, which raised the revenue ceiling to guarantee borrowing repayable by 2058, with Article 122 emergency powers as a basis. Whether such joint borrowing becomes a permanent tool is a major political controversy.",
  "eu212": "Repayments of the grant-financed portion, around 420 billion euros, begin in 2028 under the next MFF, and sharply higher interest rates have inflated servicing costs beyond original estimates. That is why the Commission proposed new own resources, including a share of emissions-trading revenue, a slice of CBAM receipts and a statistics-based corporate contribution, all still awaiting the required unanimous agreement.",
  "eu213": "Each national plan was approved by a Council implementing decision on a Commission proposal, and had to devote at least 37% of spending to climate and 20% to digital objectives; REPowerEU chapters were added after 2022 to cut Russian energy dependence. Payments required verified fulfilment of milestones, and the whole facility wound down with a final payment deadline of 31 August 2026.",
  "eu214": "Cohesion policy splits regions into three categories: less developed below 75% of average EU GDP per head, transition between 75% and 100%, and more developed above that. The main instruments are the ERDF, ESF+ and the Cohesion Fund, the last reserved for states below 90% of average GNI. Cohesion absorbs roughly a third of the budget, and co-financing rates reach 85% for the poorest regions.",
  "eu215": "InvestEU runs on a budget guarantee of about 26 billion euros intended to mobilise well over 370 billion in total investment across four policy windows: sustainable infrastructure, research and innovation, SMEs, and social investment. The EIB Group implements around three quarters of it. It succeeded the European Fund for Strategic Investments, the 2015 Juncker Plan, applying the same leverage logic of public risk-taking crowding in private capital.",
  "eu216": "SURE disbursed 98.4 billion euros to 19 member states between 2020 and 2022, financed by the EU's first large social bond issuance, and is credited with supporting over 30 million workers and self-employed people at the pandemic's peak. It was backed by voluntary member-state guarantees. Remember it as the precedent that made large-scale joint EU borrowing politically thinkable just before NextGenerationEU.",
  "eu217": "The legal basis is Article 140 TFEU: the Commission and the ECB each publish convergence reports every two years, or sooner at a member state's request. Bulgaria used exactly this route, requesting extraordinary reports in 2025 that found it compliant, after which the Council fixed the conversion rate and adoption date. The reports also verify legal convergence, meaning national central bank statutes must guarantee independence.",
  "eu218": "Denmark's euro opt-out stems from the 1992 Edinburgh Agreement, negotiated after Danish voters rejected the Maastricht Treaty, and a 2000 referendum confirmed rejection of the euro. Uniquely, the krone keeps a narrow plus or minus 2.25% band in ERM II instead of the standard 15%, so Danish monetary policy effectively shadows the ECB. Note Denmark abolished its separate defence opt-out by referendum in 2022.",
  "eu219": "The original series by Austrian designer Robert Kalina depicts seven eras of European architecture across seven denominations from 5 to 500 euros; the second, Europa series added a portrait of the mythological figure Europa in the watermark. The ECB stopped issuing 500-euro notes in 2019 over crime concerns. In 2023 the Governing Council shortlisted future themes: European culture, and rivers and birds.",
  "eu220": "The EIB Board approved the fossil-fuel lending ban in 2019, effective from the end of 2021, making it the first major multilateral bank to exit unabated oil, gas and coal energy financing. Its Climate Bank Roadmap pledged over 50% of annual financing for climate and environmental sustainability, a share achieved since 2022, plus one trillion euros of green investment support in the decade to 2030.",
  "eu221": "The revised 2020 methodology — pushed by France — grouped the roughly 35 chapters into six clusters: Fundamentals; Internal Market; Competitiveness and Inclusive Growth; Green Agenda and Sustainable Connectivity; Resources, Agriculture and Cohesion; and External Relations. Chapters 23 (judiciary and fundamental rights) and 24 (justice, freedom and security) open first and close last, and overall progress can be frozen or reversed if rule-of-law reforms backslide.",
  "eu222": "Then-President Yanukovych's refusal to sign the Association Agreement in November 2013 triggered the Euromaidan protests; the political chapters were signed in March 2014 and the economic chapters that June, with full entry into force in 2017. A DCFTA goes beyond a classic FTA: it grants partial single-market access in exchange for wholesale legislative alignment with EU rules. Ukraine, Moldova and Georgia formed the 'Association Trio' in 2021.",
  "eu223": "The SAP was launched in 1999-2000 after the Kosovo war, and the 2003 Thessaloniki European Council famously promised the Western Balkans a membership perspective — language every subsequent summit reaffirms. Stabilisation and Association Agreements are legally binding treaties establishing free trade and alignment obligations. Kosovo's 2016 SAA is unusual: it was concluded as an EU-only agreement because five member states do not recognise Kosovo.",
  "eu224": "Applied provisionally from July 2011 and formally ratified in 2015, the Korea deal scrapped nearly 99 percent of tariffs and, crucially, added chapters on services, intellectual property, public procurement and a trade and sustainable development chapter with civil society monitoring. Bilateral trade grew strongly afterwards, giving the Commission its standard evidence that FTAs pay off, and the model was replicated with Japan (2019), Singapore and Vietnam.",
  "eu225": "CETA is the textbook 'mixed agreement': trade provisions fall under EU competence and apply provisionally, while the Investment Court System needs ratification by every member state, and around a third have still not ratified. The regional parliament of Wallonia nearly blocked signature in 2016, illustrating how sub-national vetoes can paralyse mixed deals. CJEU Opinion 1/17 (2019) confirmed the ICS is compatible with EU law.",
  "eu226": "Mercosur comprises Argentina, Brazil, Paraguay and Uruguay; negotiations started in 1999, produced a political agreement in 2019, then stalled over deforestation concerns before the December 2024 conclusion in Montevideo. It would be the EU's biggest trade deal by market size, covering some 700 million people. France, backed by farming lobbies, led the opposition, so the deal was split: a mixed EU–Mercosur Partnership Agreement plus an EU-only interim Trade Agreement. Both were signed on 17 January 2026, and the interim Trade Agreement has been provisionally applied since 1 May 2026, while Parliament has asked the Court of Justice for an opinion on the agreements' compatibility with the treaties.",
  "eu227": "The scheme has three tiers: standard GSP, GSP+ (deeper cuts for vulnerable economies implementing 27 conventions on human rights, labour, environment and governance), and Everything But Arms, giving least-developed countries duty-free, quota-free access for everything except weapons. Preferences can be withdrawn for serious violations — the EU partially suspended Cambodia's EBA benefits in 2020 over political repression, the mechanism's most cited use.",
  "eu228": "The EU was a founding WTO member in 1995 and is one of the few non-state members; the Commission speaks for all 27 in Geneva under Article 207 TFEU. Since the United States began blocking Appellate Body appointments in 2017, leaving the body without a quorum from December 2019 and paralysing WTO appeals, the EU co-founded the Multi-Party Interim Appeal Arbitration Arrangement (MPIA) as a workaround and remains the leading defender of rules-based dispute settlement.",
  "eu229": "The Compass is built on four pillars — Act, Secure, Invest, Partner — and, unusually, on the EU's first-ever shared threat analysis. The Rapid Deployment Capacity, built from modified battlegroups plus enablers, was declared ready in 2025 after the EU's first live military exercises. It fed directly into later initiatives such as the 2025 defence readiness push and joint procurement instruments.",
  "eu230": "The EDF runs at about EUR 8 billion for 2021-2027, split between a research window (~EUR 2.7 billion) and a capability development window (~EUR 5.3 billion), requiring cross-border consortia of firms from several member states. It grew out of two pilots, the PADR and EDIDP. Related but distinct instruments to keep straight: ASAP (ammunition production), EDIRPA (joint procurement top-ups) and the 2025 SAFE loan scheme worth up to EUR 150 billion.",
  "eu231": "Each battlegroup is a battalion-sized force of roughly 1,500 troops, with two kept on standby in six-month rotations. Deployment requires a unanimous Council decision, and under the old 'costs lie where they fall' rule the contributing states paid most expenses — a structural disincentive now partly addressed through common funding via the European Peace Facility. The 5,000-strong Rapid Deployment Capacity is explicitly built on their reformed core.",
  "eu232": "The Temporary Protection Directive (2001/55/EC) was drafted after the Balkan displacement crises precisely to avoid swamping national asylum systems with individual claims. It grants immediate residence, work, housing, healthcare and schooling rights collectively, and beneficiaries — around four million registered Ukrainians — retain the option of applying for asylum. The Council has rolled protection forward repeatedly, most recently in July 2026 extending it until 4 March 2028, covering some 4.4 million registered beneficiaries.",
  "eu233": "Bosnia applied back in February 2016, and the Commission's 2019 opinion set out 14 key priorities on democracy, rule of law and public administration that still structure its path. In March 2024 the European Council agreed to open accession negotiations once the remaining steps are completed. The Dayton constitutional architecture, with its entity vetoes and secessionist pressure from Republika Srpska, is the chief structural brake on reform.",
  "eu234": "Kosovo declared independence in 2008; a 2010 ICJ advisory opinion found the declaration did not violate international law, but Spain, Slovakia, Cyprus, Romania and Greece still withhold recognition, making the unanimity needed for candidate status unattainable for now. The EU facilitates the Belgrade-Pristina Dialogue (Brussels Agreement 2013, Ohrid annex 2023), and Kosovars finally gained Schengen visa-free travel in January 2024, the last in the Western Balkans.",
  "eu235": "Türkiye's relationship is the EU's oldest and most layered: the Ankara Association Agreement dates from 1963, the membership application from 1987, candidate status from the 1999 Helsinki summit, and it is the only candidate inside a customs union with the EU (since 1995). Only 16 of 35 chapters were ever opened and one provisionally closed. Cooperation now runs mainly through the customs union, the 2016 migration statement and visa dialogue.",
  "eu236": "The plan's financial arm is the Reform and Growth Facility: EUR 6 billion for 2024-2027, roughly EUR 2 billion in grants and EUR 4 billion in loans, disbursed only against completed reform agendas and reversible if a country backslides. Early integration areas include joining the Single Euro Payments Area and easing goods trade. It consciously mirrors the larger Ukraine Facility, worth EUR 50 billion for 2024-2027.",
  "eu237": "To tighten patchy enforcement, the EU appointed its first Sanctions Envoy, David O'Sullivan, in 2023 to press third countries on circumvention, and a 2024 directive criminalises sanctions violations with harmonised minimum penalties across member states. National competent authorities also grant humanitarian and other exemptions. The Commission monitors implementation and can open infringement proceedings against lax member states.",
  "eu238": "NDICI-Global Europe (about EUR 79.5 billion for 2021-2027) merged more than ten previous instruments, including the formerly off-budget European Development Fund. It has three pillars — geographic (the largest, prioritising Sub-Saharan Africa and the Neighbourhood), thematic, and rapid response — plus an unallocated 'cushion' for crises. Keep it distinct from IPA III (about EUR 14.2 billion), which funds candidate countries' pre-accession reforms.",
  "eu239": "The four principles derive from international humanitarian law and UN General Assembly resolutions, and the EU anchored them in the 2007 European Consensus on Humanitarian Aid; Article 214 TFEU gives humanitarian aid its own treaty basis. Distinguish neutrality (not taking sides in hostilities) from impartiality (allocating aid solely by need). This principled separation is why humanitarian funding is never conditioned on political alignment, unlike development aid.",
  "eu240": "Created in 1988 — its first laureates were Nelson Mandela and Anatoli Marchenko — the prize is worth EUR 50,000 and is presented each December in Strasbourg. Do not confuse it with the Nobel Peace Prize (which the EU itself won in 2012) or the Charlemagne Prize awarded by the city of Aachen. Recent laureates include the Mahsa Amini movement (2023) and Venezuela's opposition leaders Machado and Gonzalez (2024).",
  "eu241": "The Executive Board comprises the President, Vice-President and four other members, appointed by the European Council acting by qualified majority on a Council recommendation after consulting Parliament and the ECB's Governing Council. Christine Lagarde's presidency runs from 2019 to 2027; her predecessors were Duisenberg, Trichet and Draghi. Compare: national central bank governors must have a minimum five-year term under the treaties.",
  "eu242": "Cedefop was one of the first decentralised agencies, founded in 1975 and originally based in Berlin before moving to Thessaloniki. Agencies are deliberately spread across member states as a matter of policy; a 2003 agreement gave every new member state a claim to host one. Related bodies: the European Training Foundation in Turin covers non-EU partner countries, and Eurofound sits in Dublin.",
  "eu243": "The three European Supervisory Authorities created in 2011 after the financial crisis are a classic memorisation set: EBA for banking (Paris since 2019), ESMA for securities and markets (Paris), and EIOPA for insurance and pensions (Frankfurt). Together with the European Systemic Risk Board at the ECB they form the European System of Financial Supervision. The relocation race for EBA saw Paris beat Dublin by lot.",
  "eu244": "Anjinho, a Portuguese former deputy ombudsman, is only the fourth holder since the office began work in 1995, after Jacob Söderman, Nikiforos Diamandouros and Emily O'Reilly, who served over a decade. The Ombudsman can be dismissed only by the Court of Justice at Parliament's request. Notable inquiries have covered Frontex, 'revolving doors' at the Commission, and access to von der Leyen's text messages.",
  "eu245": "Collegiality has practical corollaries: the College meets every Wednesday, decisions can also be taken by written procedure or by 'empowerment' of a single Commissioner for routine matters, and once adopted a decision binds all members publicly. The President assigns and can reshuffle portfolios and, since Lisbon, can force an individual Commissioner to resign, a power strengthened after the Santer crisis.",
  "eu246": "The EIB, headquartered in Luxembourg and founded by the Treaty of Rome in 1958, is owned by the member states and is the world's largest multilateral lender; it is a bank raising funds on capital markets, not a grant-giving institution. It has rebranded itself the 'EU climate bank', targeting majority green financing. Its subsidiary, the European Investment Fund, supports SMEs. Do not confuse it with the ECB or the EBRD.",
  "eu247": "EPSO, the European Personnel Selection Office, has run inter-institutional competitions ('concours') since 2003; successful candidates join reserve lists from which institutions recruit permanent officials in AD (administrator) and AST (assistant) function groups. Staff rights and duties are codified in the Staff Regulations. The Council's General Secretariat, led by a Secretary-General, supports both the Council and the European Council.",
  "eu248": "Protocol 6, agreed at Edinburgh in 1992 and annexed to the treaties by Amsterdam, fixes the seats of all institutions: Commission and Council in Brussels (with some Council sessions in Luxembourg in April, June and October), CJEU, ECA and EIB in Luxembourg, ECB in Frankfurt, Europol in The Hague. Parliament has repeatedly voted for a single seat, but only the member states, unanimously, can change the protocol.",
  "eu249": "EUIPO began in 1996 as OHIM and took its current name in 2016; it registers EU trade marks and Community designs valid across all member states with a single application, and it is fully self-financed by fees. The European Patent Office, by contrast, belongs to the 39-state European Patent Organisation, though the new EU unitary patent system, operational since 2023, builds on EPO grants with a Unified Patent Court.",
  "eu250": "The Advocate General institution was inspired by the French Conseil d'État's commissaire du gouvernement. An AG opinion is delivered some months before the judgment in complex cases, but cases raising no new point of law can be decided without one. Opinions are widely read because they argue openly, whereas the Court's single collegiate judgment discloses no dissents. Famous AG opinions shaped doctrines like direct effect.",
  "eu251": "The signing aboard the Princesse Marie-Astrid at the tripoint of France, Germany and Luxembourg was deliberately symbolic. The 1985 agreement was only a declaration of intent; the 1990 Schengen Implementing Convention created the practical machinery, including the Schengen Information System, and controls were actually abolished in 1995. Today the area covers 29 countries, including non-EU Iceland, Norway, Switzerland and Liechtenstein.",
  "eu252": "The December 1995 Madrid European Council chose 'euro' partly because 'ECU' sounded like a historical French coin and was disliked in Germany. Madrid confirmed the start of Stage Three of EMU for 1 January 1999, when exchange rates were irrevocably fixed and the euro launched as book money; notes and coins followed on 1 January 2002 in twelve countries.",
  "eu253": "The post was nicknamed 'Monsieur PESC' after the French acronym for the CFSP, and answered Henry Kissinger's famous jibe about whom to call to speak to Europe. Solana simultaneously served as Secretary-General of the Council. Learn the succession: Solana 1999-2009, Ashton 2009-2014, Mogherini 2014-2019, Borrell 2019-2024, then Kaja Kallas from December 2024.",
  "eu254": "The Ankara Agreement was signed under Article 238 EEC, the association clause, and its Additional Protocol of 1970 set out a path toward customs union that materialised only in 1995, a rarity for a non-member. Accession negotiations opened in October 2005 but were declared effectively frozen by the Council in 2018 over rule-of-law backsliding after the 2016 coup attempt.",
  "eu255": "The 6 December 1992 vote failed with 50.3% against and a majority of cantons opposed, on high turnout, after a campaign led by Christoph Blocher. Switzerland's dormant EU membership application was formally withdrawn in 2016. Bilateral packages I (1999) and II (2004) govern relations instead, and a new framework package of agreements was concluded politically in December 2024.",
  "eu256": "The Charter was mandated by the 1999 Cologne European Council and drafted by a 62-member body of national and European parliamentarians and government representatives, the first use of the convention method later copied at Laeken. It was proclaimed jointly by the Parliament, Council and Commission. Remember Protocol No 30, the interpretive protocol obtained by the UK and Poland.",
  "eu257": "Their accession treaty was signed in Luxembourg in 2005 with a safeguard clause allowing a one-year postponement, which was ultimately not used. The Cooperation and Verification Mechanism, unique to these two states, was closed for Bulgaria and Romania in September 2023. Their Schengen accession lagged even longer: air and sea borders opened in March 2024, land borders on 1 January 2025.",
  "eu258": "Reunification on 3 October 1990 added about 16 million people to the Community overnight. Special transitional measures and interim rules were adopted rapidly under Commission President Delors, who strongly backed Kohl despite hesitation from Thatcher and Mitterrand. Exam parallel: this precedent is cited in debates over hypothetical Irish reunification, and the reverse of it in Greenland's earlier territorial exit.",
  "eu259": "The 1970 Treaty of Luxembourg accompanied the shift from national contributions to 'own resources' such as customs duties and agricultural levies; the 1975 Treaty of Brussels added Parliament's right to reject the whole budget and grant discharge, and created the Court of Auditors. Parliament flexed these muscles by rejecting entire budgets in 1979 and 1984. Legislative co-decision came only with Maastricht.",
  "eu260": "Agreed in January 1966 by foreign ministers, the Compromise was a political 'agreement to disagree' with no legal force, and it was never incorporated into the treaties. Its practical effect was to make unanimity the norm even where the treaties allowed majority voting, slowing legislation badly until the mid-1980s. Its spirit occasionally resurfaces, as in the 1994 Ioannina compromise on voting thresholds.",
  "eu261": "The EAW was adopted as Framework Decision 2002/584 after 9/11 and abolished the political phase of extradition and, for 32 listed offence categories, the double-criminality check. A final decision on execution must normally be taken within 60 days of arrest (10 days if the person consents, extendable by 30 in exceptional cases), and the surrender itself within 10 days of that decision, and states can no longer refuse to hand over their own nationals. Case law (Aranyosi and Căldăraru, 2016; LM, 2018) permits refusal where the person faces inhuman detention conditions or an unfair trial due to rule-of-law failings.",
  "eu262": "EU citizenship was created by the Maastricht Treaty in 1993 and is 'additional to' national citizenship, never replacing it (Articles 20 to 24 TFEU). The Court called it 'destined to be the fundamental status' of nationals in Grzelczyk (2001), and in Zambrano (2011) protected the residence of third-country parents of EU-citizen children. Consular protection is fleshed out by Directive 2015/637 — useful because only a few capitals host embassies of all member states.",
  "eu263": "The petition right sits in Articles 24 and 227 TFEU and Article 44 of the Charter, and unlike the ECI it is open to companies and to any resident, not just citizens, with no signature threshold. Petitions have real effects: they can prompt Commission infringement inquiries, own-initiative reports, and fact-finding visits. Do not confuse the addressee with the Ombudsman, who handles maladministration by EU institutions rather than general EU-law grievances.",
  "eu264": "French became the deliberation language when the Court began in 1952, reflecting the era's diplomacy, and judges deliberate alone, without interpreters, in strict secrecy with no dissenting opinions ever published. The language of procedure is chosen by the applicant in direct actions but is the referring court's language in preliminary rulings. Since 2022 the Court also publishes English translations more systematically, but authenticity stays with the procedural language.",
  "eu265": "Ordinary revision under Article 48 TEU starts with proposals from any government, the Parliament or the Commission; the European Council may convene a Convention or, with Parliament's consent, skip it for minor changes. A simplified revision procedure exists for internal policies but cannot increase EU competences — used in 2011 to add the ESM legal base to Article 136 TFEU. Ratification failures have real bite: French and Dutch referendums sank the Constitutional Treaty in 2005.",
  "eu266": "These penalties were introduced by the Maastricht Treaty and are calculated from a base rate multiplied by coefficients for seriousness, duration, and the state's capacity to pay (the 'n factor'). The first penalty hit Greece in 2000 over toxic waste in Crete; Poland was fined EUR 500 000 a day over the Turów mine and a record EUR 1 million a day in 2021 for not suspending the Supreme Court's Disciplinary Chamber, both in interim proceedings. Since Commission v France (2005) the Court can combine a lump sum and a periodic penalty.",
  "eu267": "Article 107(1) TFEU defines aid through four cumulative criteria: state resources, selective advantage, distortion of competition, and effect on trade between member states. New aid must be notified and cannot be implemented before approval — the 'standstill obligation' — and unlawful aid is recovered with interest, up to ten years back. The headline case is Apple: in September 2024 the Court of Justice definitively ordered Ireland to recover 13 billion euros.",
  "eu268": "Express treaty derogations (Articles 36, 45(3) and 52 TFEU) are interpreted strictly and can justify even discriminatory measures, while court-made 'overriding reasons in the public interest' — consumer protection, environment, road safety — apply only to indistinctly applicable rules. Purely economic aims never qualify. The state must prove necessity and the absence of less restrictive alternatives, and invoking public policy requires a genuine, sufficiently serious threat to a fundamental interest of society.",
  "eu269": "The classic authority is Variola (1973), where the Court barred Italy from reproducing a regulation in national legislation, to preserve its Community character. Direct applicability concerns how a norm enters the national legal order; direct effect concerns whether an individual can rely on it in court, which still requires the provision to be clear, precise and unconditional. Regulation provisions needing implementing measures may lack direct effect despite being directly applicable.",
  "eu270": "Map the three channels precisely: petitions go to Parliament's PETI committee about EU-law matters affecting you; Ombudsman complaints target maladministration by EU institutions, not national ones, and must be lodged within two years; the ECI asks the Commission to propose legislation. The Ombudsman post was created by the Maastricht Treaty and the office can also open own-initiative inquiries. All three channels are free of charge and available in any official language.",
  "eu271": "The revised eIDAS regulation (eIDAS 2) entered into force in May 2024, with wallets due to be offered by member states around the end of 2026. Very large platforms and sectors like banking must accept the wallet for login. Design principles to remember: selective disclosure, meaning you prove your age without revealing your birth date, a privacy dashboard, and no tracking of wallet use.",
  "eu272": "EMFA is grounded in internal-market competence, Article 114 TFEU, a novelty for media policy, and creates the European Board for Media Services replacing the older ERGA network of regulators. It responds to concerns about media capture in states like Hungary and Poland and the Pegasus spyware scandal. It also gives media outlets special safeguards before very large platforms can remove their content.",
  "eu273": "The 90% for 2040 figure comes from the European Scientific Advisory Board on Climate Change, which recommended 90-95%. The amendment to the Climate Law was adopted in March 2026 and includes contested flexibilities, notably allowing a limited share of high-quality international carbon credits from 2036. The 2040 target also anchors the EU's NDC under the Paris Agreement, so delays have international repercussions.",
  "eu274": "ETS2 is upstream: fuel suppliers, not households, surrender allowances, but costs pass through to heating and petrol prices, which is politically sensitive. A price-containment mechanism releases extra allowances if prices exceed roughly EUR 45 per tonne in early years. The linked Social Climate Fund, about EUR 65 billion from 2026, is financed largely by ETS2 revenue to support vulnerable households and micro-enterprises.",
  "eu275": "TEN-T dates from the Maastricht era but the 2024 revision made deadlines binding and geopolitics prominent: four extended corridors now reach Ukraine and Moldova, while cooperation with Russia and Belarus was terminated. Requirements include 160 km/h minimum passenger rail speeds on core lines, European standard gauge for new lines, and urban nodes preparing sustainable mobility plans. It connects to the military-mobility agenda for moving troops.",
  "eu276": "CE marking flows from the 'New Approach' of 1985: legislation sets only essential requirements, harmonised European standards from CEN and CENELEC give presumption of conformity, and market-surveillance authorities police compliance afterwards. This model underpins mutual recognition across the single market. A common trap: the near-identical 'China Export' logo has no legal meaning, and CE marking applies only to product categories covered by EU harmonisation law.",
  "eu277": "Distinguish the labels: PDO (protected designation of origin) requires all production steps in the defined area, as with feta or Parmigiano Reggiano, while PGI (protected geographical indication) requires only one step linked to the area. There is also TSG for traditional recipes and, since 2023, GI protection for craft and industrial products like Murano glass. Geographical indications are a recurring sticking point in EU trade negotiations.",
  "eu278": "EU4Health is roughly ten times larger than its predecessor health programme, a scale shift justified by COVID-19. Flagship initiatives it supports include Europe's Beating Cancer Plan, the Pharmaceutical Strategy and the European Health Data Space. Money flows mainly through work programmes managed by the HaDEA executive agency. Remember Article 168 TFEU: the EU complements national policies, so the programme funds cooperation, not hospitals.",
  "eu279": "Launched in 2004, the Single European Sky created functional airspace blocks and the SESAR technology programme, but member states resist ceding airspace sovereignty, partly for military reasons, and air-traffic controllers' unions oppose consolidation. The SES2+ reform was agreed in 2024. Fragmentation is costly: longer routes burn extra fuel, so the initiative is also framed as a climate measure potentially cutting aviation emissions by around 10%.",
  "eu280": "Adopted in 2024, the CRA is the first law worldwide to impose horizontal cybersecurity rules on hardware and software placed on the EU market, with main obligations applying from 2027. Manufacturers must handle vulnerabilities, report actively exploited ones to ENISA within 24 hours, and state a support period. Open-source software developed non-commercially is largely exempt after intense lobbying. Compliance is signalled through CE marking.",
  "eu281": "MiCA (Regulation 2023/1114) phased in during 2024: stablecoin rules, covering asset-referenced and e-money tokens, applied from June, and the licensing regime for crypto-asset service providers from December, with an EU passport for authorised firms. NFTs and fully decentralised finance largely fall outside it. Pair it in your notes with DORA, the digital operational resilience regulation for the financial sector, applying from January 2025.",
  "eu282": "Frankfurt beat eight other cities in February 2024 in the first EU agency seat contest where Parliament voted alongside the Council. AMLA anchors a wider package that includes a directly applicable single rulebook regulation and an EU-wide cap of 10,000 euros on large cash payments. It began building up in 2025 and takes over direct supervision of roughly forty high-risk cross-border financial entities from 2028.",
  "eu283": "The 2024 regulation amends the SEPA framework with staggered deadlines: euro-area banks had to receive instant credit transfers from January 2025 and send them from October 2025, with non-euro-area providers following in 2027. It also mandates a free payee-verification service matching the recipient's name and IBAN to fight fraud, and daily sanctions screening instead of per-transaction checks that caused rejections.",
  "eu284": "The directive, adopted unanimously in December 2022 under Article 115 TFEU and applying from 2024, transposes the OECD/G20 Inclusive Framework deal agreed by around 140 jurisdictions. It covers multinational and large domestic groups with consolidated revenue of at least 750 million euros, using top-up taxes to reach the 15% floor. Pillar One, reallocating taxing rights to market countries, remains stalled internationally.",
  "eu285": "In the 2021-2027 MFF, agriculture and cohesion each take roughly 30% of spending; the CAP alone consumed about 70% of the budget in the 1980s, so the long-term trend is decline in favour of research, digital, defence and migration. Know the CAP's two pillars: the EAGF financing direct payments and market measures, and the EAFRD financing rural development.",
  "eu286": "Two ceilings matter: payments at 1.40% of GNI and commitments slightly higher at 1.46%, plus a temporary extra 0.6 points of headroom that exists purely to guarantee NextGenerationEU borrowing, bringing the total near 2% until the debt is repaid. The gap between the ceiling and actual spending is the headroom that reassures investors the EU can always service its bonds.",
  "eu287": "The discharge procedure rests on Article 319 TFEU: the Council recommends by qualified majority, then Parliament decides, guided by its Budgetary Control (CONT) committee and the Court of Auditors' findings. Parliament may grant, postpone or refuse discharge, and it uses the same power over agencies and other institutions, having repeatedly postponed discharge for bodies like Frontex to force reforms. Refusal is a grave political sanction.",
  "eu288": "The symbol was unveiled by the Commission in December 1996, ahead of the 1999 launch, and the currency's name itself was chosen at the December 1995 Madrid European Council. The official ISO code is EUR, and one euro divides into 100 cent. Note that the glyph's placement before or after the amount varies by language convention, and the symbol is not used in EU legal acts, which write 'EUR'.",
  "eu289": "The April 2024 package comprises two regulations and a directive replacing the old preventive and corrective arm rules. Each state agrees a net expenditure path based on a Commission debt sustainability analysis, extendable from four to seven years in exchange for reforms and investment. Two safeguards bite: minimum average annual debt reduction of 1% (or 0.5%) of GDP, and a deficit resilience margin of 1.5% of GDP.",
  "eu290": "Greece received three programmes: the 2010 bilateral Greek Loan Facility, a 2012 EFSF programme accompanied by the largest sovereign debt restructuring in history (private-sector involvement cutting around 100 billion euros of debt), and a 2015-2018 ESM programme. Surveillance was run by the troika of Commission, ECB and IMF. Greece regained investment-grade credit ratings in 2023, a decade after near-Grexit.",
  "eu291": "Adopted in December 2020, the regime targets acts like genocide, crimes against humanity, torture and systematic repression, using asset freezes and travel bans; unlike the US Magnitsky Act, it does not cover corruption, which EPSO questions sometimes probe. Listings still require Council unanimity. Its first use in March 2021 sanctioned Chinese officials over Xinjiang — the EU's first sanctions on China since the 1989 arms embargo.",
  "eu292": "The instrument entered into force in December 2023 and was inspired by China's informal trade blockade of Lithuania after Vilnius allowed a 'Taiwanese' representative office in 2021. Procedurally, the Commission investigates and the Council determines the existence of coercion by qualified majority — deliberately avoiding a unanimity veto. Countermeasures can span tariffs, services, intellectual property rights and exclusion from public procurement; its main value is deterrence.",
  "eu293": "Trade defence instruments implement WTO rules: duties normally last five years, subject to expiry reviews, and follow Commission investigations usually triggered by industry complaints. Most measures target Chinese products such as steel and aluminium. The highest-profile recent case is the October 2024 countervailing duties on Chinese battery electric vehicles, adopted despite German opposition — proof that these decisions do not need unanimity: under the examination procedure the Commission's duties stand unless a qualified majority of member states votes them down.",
  "eu294": "The EU is represented at G20 summits by the Commission President and the European Council President jointly, while Germany, France and Italy attend as national members and Spain as a permanent guest; the African Union became a full member in 2023, ending the EU's status as the only non-state member. In the smaller G7 the EU also participates fully, though it never holds the rotating presidency.",
  "eu295": "EOMs are financed under the thematic human rights strand of NDICI-Global Europe and operate under the 2005 UN Declaration of Principles for International Election Observation, combining long-term and short-term observers. They never deploy inside EU member states — election observation in Europe is the job of the OSCE's ODIHR, a frequent confusion. Follow-up missions later assess whether the host implemented the recommendations.",
  "eu296": "The visa lists are set by Regulation 2018/1806; visa-free entry means stays of up to 90 days in any 180-day period, without work rights. A suspension mechanism allows the EU to revoke the privilege, as it did for Vanuatu over its golden-passport scheme. Visa-exempt travellers will in addition need an ETIAS pre-travel authorisation once that system starts; the Entry/Exit System completed its rollout in April 2026, but ETIAS has slipped past its late-2026 target and is now expected in 2027, with the exact start date still to be announced.",
  "eu297": "Cooperation began operationally with the 2003 Berlin Plus arrangements, letting EU operations draw on NATO assets. The three joint declarations were signed in Warsaw (2016), Brussels (2018) and January 2023, expanding into resilience, space and critical infrastructure after the Nord Stream sabotage. Both bodies stress a 'single set of forces'. Only four EU states remain outside NATO: Ireland, Austria, Malta and Cyprus.",
  "eu298": "The label was coined in April 2020 to package the EU's global COVID-19 response, bundling the institutions, the 27 member states, the European Investment Bank and the EBRD as one donor. It works through 'Team Europe Initiatives' that pool funding around joint priorities per country or region. The approach maximises visibility against competitors like China and underpins the EUR 300 billion Global Gateway headline figure.",
  "eu299": "The treaty basis is Article 221 TFEU. Before Lisbon these were 'Commission delegations' handling mainly trade and aid, while the rotating presidency's national embassy chaired local EU coordination; now the EU Head of Delegation, an ambassador presenting credentials to the host state, performs that role permanently. Consular protection of citizens remains a member-state competence, though delegations can support evacuations in crises.",
  "eu300": "Operating since 2013 within DG ECHO, the ERCC serves the Union Civil Protection Mechanism, which includes all member states plus around ten participating states such as Norway, Iceland, Türkiye and Ukraine. The rescEU reserve, created in 2019 after the devastating 2017-18 wildfires, gives it EU-owned aircraft, medical stockpiles and shelter capacity. Its response to Russia's war on Ukraine became the mechanism's largest and longest operation ever."
};
